\par\par \uc2 REVIEW & OUTLOOK (Editorial)\par \b \uc2 Biden Grounds Spirit Airlines\b0\par\par\uc2 507 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 November 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A16\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Meet the latest victim of Biden Administration antitrust policy: \uc2 Spirit Airlines\uc2 . \uc2 Spirit\uc2  declared bankruptcy Monday after the Justice Department cut off a lifeline by blocking its merger with \uc2 JetBlue Airways\uc2 . Too bad the government won't compensate the workers, flyers and creditors harmed by its blunder.\par \par \uc2 Spirit\uc2  has run up $3.3 billion in debt as it slashed prices to attract customers. The scrappy discount airline last decade spurred more industry competition by unbundling fares, charging more for carry-on baggage, and scrapping amenities. But legacy airlines followed, and \uc2 Spirit\uc2  had too few airport gates, pilots and planes to compete effectively.\par \par \uc2 Its ultra-low fares became financially unsustainable after a new labor agreement boosted pilot pay by some 34% over two years. \uc2 JetBlue\uc2 's $3.8 billion buyout would have rescued \uc2 Spirit\uc2  and strengthened both airlines' ability to go toe-to-toe with legacy carriers. The combined company would have been the fifth largest U.S. carrier, though its market share (10.5%) would still have trailed Delta (17.7%), American (17.2%), Southwest (16.9%) and United (16.1%).\par \par \uc2 Justice antitrust chief \uc2 Jonathan Kanter\uc2  never lets economic reality interfere with his anti-business crusades. DOJ challenged the merger as anti-competitive. Never mind that its argument contradicted DOJ's claim in its 2021 lawsuit against \uc2 JetBlue\uc2 's alliance in the Northeast with \uc2 American Airlines\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In January federal Judge William Young sided with DOJ even as he agreed that "an expansion of all aspects of \uc2 JetBlue\uc2 's business -- including network, fleet, and loyalty program -- would allow for more vigorous competition with the Big Four, which carry most passengers in the country." He also acknowledged \uc2 Spirit\uc2  was struggling financially.\par \par \uc2 While \uc2 JetBlue\uc2  and \uc2 Spirit\uc2  initially appealed the decision, they walked away from the merger in March owing to the legal uncertainty that made it difficult to move ahead with other business plans. The Justice Department hailed the deal's termination as "a victory for U.S. travelers who deserve lower prices and better choices."\par \par \uc2 Now passengers can look forward to fewer choices and higher prices as a result of \uc2 Spirit\uc2 's bankruptcy. \uc2 Spirit\uc2  furloughed hundreds of pilots this autumn to shave costs. Its Chapter 11 filing will let it restructure debts while continuing to operate, but it's hard to see how \uc2 Spirit\uc2  can emerge in a stronger position to compete with legacy carriers.\par \par \uc2 Some of its assets including planes may have to be sold to other airlines to pay off creditors. Post-bankruptcy, it will still lack the planes, gates and pilots to go toe-to-toe with the giants. Meantime, \uc2 JetBlue\uc2 's debt this summer was downgraded further into junk status because it is also struggling to compete against the Big Four, especially on the East Coast.\par \par \uc2 DOJ's other lawsuit blocking JetBlue's Northeast Alliance with American has benefited no one other than United and Delta. Call it the Biden antitrust paradox. In the name of protecting competition, regulators are helping big companies get bigger. As usual, consumers and workers will pay.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020241119ekbj0000k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
