\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: With Cuts Eyed, Federal Workers Stand at 2.3 Million\b0\par\par\uc2 By Lauren Weber, Peter Santilli and Inti Pacheco \par \uc2 480 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 November 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Corrections & Amplifications\par \par \uc2 The Department of Homeland Security is the second largest cabinet-level agency in terms of civilian workers. A U.S. News article on Monday about federal-government workers incorrectly said third largest.\par \par \uc2 (WSJ November 20, 2024)\par \par \uc2 (END)\par \par \uc2 There are 2.3 million Americans working for the federal government in civilian jobs, a tally that has steadily climbed as control of the White House has shifted between parties and presidents.\par \par \uc2 They constitute less than 2% of the total U.S. workforce. They work as everything from nurses in Veterans Affairs hospitals and park rangers in Yellowstone to guards in federal prisons and the 19 employees of the Nuclear Waste Technical Review Board. About 80% of them work outside of the Washington, D.C., region.\par \par \uc2 Many of the jobs could become targets of the proposed Department of Government Efficiency, the entity President-elect \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has said will trim costs under the direction of billionaire industrialist \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  and biotech entrepreneur \uc2 Vivek Ramaswamy\uc2 . Musk has told people -- possibly in jest -- that more than half of the government's employees should be fired.\par \par \uc2 Roughly 70% of the civilian roles are in military- or security-related agencies. Veterans Affairs has the most civilian workers, mainly because it operates hundreds of hospitals and clinics. Homeland Security, created in 2002, is now the third largest. The Education Department, with 4,425 workers, is the smallest.\par \par \uc2 This chart shows civilian employees only and excludes active military personnel or reservists of the Army, Navy and Air Force. (See accompanying illustration -- WSJ Nov. 18, 2024) It also excludes the Postal Service, which has more than 500,000 workers but whose operations are self-funded, and temporary \uc2 U.S. Census Bureau\uc2  jobs, which spike each decade.\par \par \uc2 The amount spent on annual payroll for federal civilian workers was about $213 billion as of March 2024, according to an analysis of data from the Office of Personnel Management. Workers in the Education Department had a median salary of $118,000 -- the highest. The lowest median salary -- below $60,000 -- was in the Treasury Department, which has many clerical jobs.\par \par \uc2 The most common occupations are in the medical field, accounting for about 15% of the total. There are more than 360,000 physicians, nurses and other public-health workers. Another 15% of federal workers are in administrative or clerical roles. Roughly 6% are in engineering roles, and another 5% are accounting.\par \par \uc2 Musk and Ramaswamy have a year and a half to complete their work, Trump has said.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Tesla\uc2  chief, who slashed 80% of \uc2 Twitter\uc2  employees after he bought the social-media platform now known as X, previously said he hopes to cut federal spending by at least $2 trillion. In fiscal 2024, the government spent roughly $6.8 trillion.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw498\pich480\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal7470\pichgoal7200
