\par\par \b \uc2 EXCHANGE --- GM Cuts 1,000 Jobs in Latest Effort To Reduce Expenses Amid EV Push\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ben Glickman \par \uc2 419 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 November 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B10\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 General Motors has cut 1,000 jobs, according to a person familiar with the reductions, marking the latest effort by the automaker to trim costs as it ramps up sales in unprofitable electric vehicles.\par \par \uc2 The Detroit-based owner of Chevrolet and Cadillac confirmed Friday in a statement that the company had made a small number of team reductions.\par \par \uc2 The cuts come months after a previous round of layoffs, focused on the company's software and services division, affected more than a thousand employees. Last year, about 5,000 salaried workers and executives accepted voluntary buyout offers.\par \par \uc2 Globally, automakers have been cutting jobs as part of broader efforts to shed costs. Volkswagen, GM, Ford Motor and other car companies are under pressure from an uncertain economic and trade outlook, competition from China and the need to invest billions of dollars on a transition to electric cars.\par \par \uc2 VW, BMW and Ford each posted a drop in third-quarter profits, pointing to factors such as weakness in China and higher warranty costs. Each cited the need to cut expenses, and the union representing VW said the German auto giant intends to close three factories and lay off tens of thousands of workers.\par \par \uc2 GM has largely bucked the downward trend, bolstered by continued strength from its lucrative truck and SUV sales in the North American market, by far its largest. Company executives have said previous cost-cutting efforts have helped sustain profitability.\par \par \uc2 The American automaker has largely stuck with a rollout of new battery-powered models, even as some other U.S. manufacturers have delayed or scrapped certain plans.\par \par \uc2 Sales of the company's EVs are picking up, the company said with third-quarter results last month, but those vehicles are still expected to lose money in 2025.\par \par \uc2 The company has targeted $2 billion worth of cost cuts by the end of the year, which it has said it is on track to meet.\par \par \uc2 "In order to win in this competitive market, we need to optimize for speed and excellence," GM said in a statement. "This includes operating with efficiency, ensuring we have the right team structure, and focusing on our top priorities as a business."\par \par \uc2 The cuts confirmed Friday were previously reported by CNBC. GM's latest results were boosted by sales of its profitable gas-powered trucks and sport-utility vehicles, leading the company's profits to surge past Wall Street's expectations.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020241116ekbg00021\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
