\par\par \b \uc2 Business News: Advance Auto Parts to Close 500 Stores --- Car-parts retailer announces overhaul after a surprise quarterly loss\b0\par\par\uc2 By Dean Seal \par \uc2 586 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 November 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Advance Auto Parts said it would close hundreds of stores and cut jobs after posting a surprising quarterly loss and forecasting revenue below market expectations.\par \par \uc2 The aftermarket car-parts retailer said Thursday that a new restructuring plan calls for the closure of about 500 stores, about 200 independent locations and four distribution centers by mid-2025. The company also is cutting jobs, though it didn't say how many.\par \par \uc2 The overhaul is expected to cost between $350 million and $750 million. This includes $45 million of severance costs and benefits; $100 million to $250 million from lease terminations; $130 million to $150 million in other closure-related costs; and $75 million to $350 million in asset-related charges.\par \par \uc2 The restructuring was approved by the board, which added three new members earlier this year at the behest of activist hedge fund Third Point. Under the new board's leadership, Advance Auto Parts also sold its Worldpac subsidiary to Carlyle Group for $1.5 billion in its effort to slim down and improve profitability.\par \par \uc2 Shares rose 0.6% to $41.20 on Thursday. The stock is still down 32% since the start of the year, but it is moving away from a 15-year low hit late last month.\par \par \uc2 The Raleigh, N.C., company separately reported Thursday that its third-quarter loss narrowed to $6.01 million, or 10 cents a share, from a loss of $62 million, or $1.04 a share, a year ago. Analysts polled by FactSet had expected the company to swing to a profit of 49 cents a share.\par \par \uc2 Sales slid 3% to $2.15 billion, missing analyst forecasts for $2.62 billion, according to FactSet. Comparable sales declined 2.3%. Margins were dented by higher wages for the company's front-line employees, partially offset by a pullback in marketing costs.\par \par \uc2 The retailer appears to have lost market share during the quarter, Davidson analysts said in a research note. The company's adjusted operating margin and earnings were both weaker than expected, even when excluding dents from hurricane impacts and the CrowdStrike outage, they said.\par \par \uc2 Advance Auto Parts offered a new version of its fiscal 2024 outlook that excludes discontinued operations, including its since-sold Worldpac subsidiary. The implied guidance for unadjusted fourth-quarter sales still looks weaker than Wall Street analysts had been expecting, Davidson analysts said, though they add that the consensus may not have fully adjusted out the Worldpac impact yet.\par \par \uc2 The company also gave preliminary guidance for $8.4 billion to $8.6 billion in sales for fiscal 2025, with a gain of 0.5% to 1.5% in comparable sales, which adjust for store openings and closings.\par \par \uc2 The estimate comes below the current $10.79 billion revenue forecast from analysts surveyed by FactSet.\par \par \uc2 Management is trying to play the long game despite some near-term pain with a plan to boost its earnings power significantly by 2027, according to the Davidson analysts.\par \par \uc2 Along with its store closures, the company is consolidating large distribution centers and opening new market hubs in the next few years.\par \par \uc2 The company laid out 2027 financial objectives, saying it aims for $9 billion in sales that year, positive low-single-digit percentage growth in comparable sales and margins at 7%. That would be up from less than 1% this year and an expected 2% to 3% next year, Davidson analysts said.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw262\pich467\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3930\pichgoal7005
