\par\par \b \uc2 Chip Maker AMD to Cut Global Headcount By 4%\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sabela Ojea \par \uc2 268 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 November 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Advanced Micro Devices is reducing its global workforce by 4% after reporting that most of the growth it sees comes from its AI chips and other products for data centers.\par \par \uc2 Shares of the chip maker declined 3% to $139.30 in Wednesday's trading. The stock has dropped nearly 6% in the year to date, though it is up 19% over the past 12 months.\par \par \uc2 The chip maker better known as AMD on Wednesday said this round of layoffs is part of a shift in its broader business strategy.\par \par \uc2 "As a part of aligning our resources with our largest growth opportunities, we are taking a number of targeted steps that will unfortunately result in reducing our global workforce by approximately 4%," the company said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 The layoffs would affect about 1,040 employees. AMD had a global workforce of 26,000 employees as of Dec. 31, according to its latest annual filing with the Securities and Exchange Commission.\par \par \uc2 The decision comes shortly after Intel said its previously planned layoffs would total 16,500, up from a previous target of roughly 15,000.\par \par \uc2 It also follows a sharp rise in AMD sales for the third quarter, but a slightly weaker-than-expected outlook for the fourth quarter on what analysts saw as a potential slowdown on its business momentum.\par \par \uc2 Still, in a call with analysts late in October, Chief Executive Lisa Su raised her projection for AMD's sales of AI chips this year to $5 billion from a $4.5 billion forecast in July.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020241114ekbe0000w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
