\par\par \uc2 The Property Report\par \b \uc2 AI Fuels Tech Demand for Office Space --- Leasing jumped in the third quarter to its highest level in nearly three years\b0\par\par\uc2 By Peter Grant \par \uc2 461 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 November 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B6\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Technology companies' leasing of office space jumped in the third quarter to its highest level in nearly three years, reflecting the growth of artificial-intelligence firms and job growth in the tech industry.\par \par \uc2 Tech firms leased 9.9 million square feet of U.S. office space during the third quarter. That was up from about eight million in the second quarter and the highest amount since the fourth quarter of 2021, according to a new report from commercial real-estate services firm CBRE Group.\par \par \uc2 Hiring by tech companies has also edged higher, resulting in an uptick in office demand.\par \par \uc2 Job growth was up 1% through July, compared with all of 2023 when it rose 0.3%.\par \par \uc2 That is still well off the 5.4% growth in 2021, CBRE said.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. office market remains in a glut, in part because many tech companies are taking less space when signing new leases because of hybrid workplace strategies.\par \par \uc2 About 30 million people in the U.S., or 19.5% of employed people, worked remotely at least some of the time in 2024, said the report.\par \par \uc2 That was nearly double the 10% who worked remotely in 2020.\par \par \uc2 "Some companies are moving [to new locations] to create more efficient space," said Colin Yasukochi, head of CBRE's Tech Insights Center. "But they're taking less of it."\par \par \uc2 Tech companies have been the dominant tenant in West Coast cities such as Seattle and San Francisco.\par \par \uc2 By 2021, these companies came to rival those in the finance industry as Manhattan's biggest user of office space. That demand continued even during the pandemic but began to wane last year.\par \par \uc2 A growing slice of office-leasing demand is coming from new AI companies like OpenAI and Anthropic that are growing in cities such as San Francisco, New York, Seattle and Boston.\par \par \uc2 AI firms backed by venture-capital firms have leased 6 million square feet in the top six markets since 2019, said Yasukochi.\par \par \uc2 That figure doesn't include expansion by AI businesses that are part of big technology companies like Google and Microsoft, he said.\par \par \uc2 "We see AI as a huge catalyst going forward," Yasukochi said.\par \par \uc2 In recent months, some high-profile companies such as Amazon.com have called workers back to offices five days a week. But so far there have been few repercussions from that change in office leasing markets, Yasukochi said.\par \par \uc2 "Right now, because the economy is a little bit tenuous, companies aren't going out and leasing more space in anticipation of more people coming back," he said. "They're going to see how it goes."\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020241113ekbd00014\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
