\par\par \uc2 Technology\par \b \uc2 DNA-Test Firm Cuts Staff 40%, Shuts Unit\b0\par\par\uc2 By Rolfe Winkler and Sabela Ojea \par \uc2 232 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 November 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 DNA-testing firm 23andMe said on Monday that it was firing roughly 40% of its workforce, over 200 people, as Chief Executive Anne Wojcicki seeks to stabilize her money-losing business.\par \par \uc2 As part of the restructuring, the company said it was closing its drug-development arm, which had been conducting human trials for two of the company's advanced drug candidates. The company said it would wind down those trials and try to sell those two prospects.\par \par \uc2 Wojcicki for years touted drug development as central to the company's strategy to profit from the trove of data that consumers send in to learn about their ancestry. In August, 23andMe said it would close its sister drug-discovery unit, which had been tasked with identifying new drug candidates.\par \par \uc2 23andMe still hopes that pharmaceutical companies will license its genetics data to help develop their own drugs. It has been seeking additional partners since it announced that a prior deal with pharmaceuticals giant GSK would no longer be exclusive. A year later, it hasn't announced any others.\par \par \uc2 The company has undergone five rounds of layoffs since the beginning of 2023. Back then, it had more than 800 employees. After this round of cuts, that number falls to about 300. It said Monday's restructuring would save $35 million annually.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020241112ekbc0001s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
