\par\par \uc2 REVIEW & OUTLOOK (Editorial)\par \b \uc2 The Federal Workforce Boom\b0\par\par\uc2 613 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 November 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A16\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Remember when \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  in 1996 declared that the era of big government was over? Well, it's back and bigger than ever. While neither \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  nor \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is talking about the need to shrink Washington, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  to his credit is, and it sure needs shrinking.\par \par \uc2 The left is attacking Mr. Musk, who has volunteered to run a government efficiency commission if Mr. Trump wins. He's floated the idea that he could cut federal spending by $2 trillion. That's impossible without the entitlement reform that Mr. Trump opposes. But Mr. Musk's reform ambition is much needed in government.\par \par \uc2 The nearby chart shows the growth in the federal bureaucracy over time (See accompanying chart -- WSJ Nov. 6, 2025). After rising modestly in the first Trump years, federal employment spiked in 2020 owing to temporary workers hired as part of the decennial Census survey. The federal workforce declined in 2021 but remained above pre-pandemic levels, and it has exploded in the Biden era.\par \par \uc2 Since January 2021, the federal workforce has increased by about 120,800 employees. Washington has added as many workers in the last two years as during the prior 13. One reason is Veterans Affairs hospitals, which have staffed up to treat aging veterans. But employment has ballooned across nearly the entire government, as quarterly data from the Office of Personnel Management show.\par \par \uc2 Between December 2020 and March 2024 (the latest data), employment has increased sharply at the \uc2 Environmental Protection Agency\uc2  (9.4%), Agriculture Department (9.6%), Department of Housing and Urban Development (10.7%), \uc2 Internal Revenue Service\uc2  (14%), Energy Department (14.8%), State Department (18.4%) and Health and Human Services (18.7%).\par \par \uc2 Independent agencies have also mushroomed, including the \uc2 Consumer Financial Protection Bureau\uc2  (9.8%), \uc2 Securities and Exchange Commission\uc2  (11.1%) and \uc2 Federal Trade Commission\uc2  (11.6%). Even the \uc2 White House Council of Economic Advisers\uc2  has increased by a third during the Biden Presidency, not that economic policy has improved. A rare Biden-era exception is Defense and the armed forces (-1.2%), despite an increasingly dangerous world.\par \par \uc2 What are all of these new bureaucrats doing? Writing new rules and mandates for the rest of America to live by, and to punish citizens who don't comply. Hardly a day goes when we don't field a call about a new costly regulation or enforcement action.\par \par \uc2 New rules can run to hundreds of pages and require battalions of lawyers, accountants and consultants to comply. The ranks of environmental consultants have increased about 20% from pre-pandemic levels, versus 5% for all jobs in the private economy. Government lawyers and consultants don't improve productivity, products or living standards.\par \par \uc2 All of this would be a ripe target for Mr. Musk, who has endured the burdens of an overgrown bureaucracy. His businesses have been besieged by an alphabet soup of agencies including the \uc2 FTC\uc2 , SEC, \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2 , Transportation Department, \uc2 Federal Aviation Administration\uc2 , \uc2 Fish and Wildlife Service\uc2  and \uc2 Federal Communications Commission\uc2 , among others.\par \par \uc2 Feeding on bigger government are leftwing groups like the Sierra Club and Open Markets Institute. Employment at social advocacy and environmental organizations has increased by about 20% during the Biden years, according to the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2 . The Administration has channeled grants and money collected from enforcement to such groups so they can file more lawsuits against companies or uncooperative states.\par \par \uc2 We didn't know the election results as we published this Tuesday evening. But if Mr. Trump wins, the growth of Leviathan under President Biden means Mr. Musk would have thousands of opportunities for government reform or program cuts. The country would be better for it.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020241106ekb60001a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
