\par\par \b \uc2 Boeing Machinists Return to Work Today\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sharon Terlep \par \uc2 654 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 November 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's machinists will begin returning to work on Wednesday, ending a paralyzing strike and restarting the plane maker's factories.\par \par \uc2 Members of \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's largest union voted in favor of a new contract that delivers a 38% wage increase over four years for 33,000 machinists in the Pacific Northwest. Their strike, which began on Sept. 13, has idled factories that make the company's bestselling 737 jets.\par \par \uc2 The walkout has strained the finances of a critical American manufacturer that has been hobbled by quality problems and deep losses in its commercial and defense operations. Chief Executive \uc2 Kelly Ortberg\uc2  is cutting 17,000 jobs while raising more than $24 billion in equity to keep the company afloat.\par \par \uc2 The strike is estimated to have cost $11.6 billion, according to economist \uc2 Patrick Anderson\uc2 , making it the costliest stoppage in decades, exceeding recent strikes by the \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  and other unions. His estimate includes lost wages, losses to \uc2 Boeing\uc2 , its suppliers and customers.\par \par \uc2 Shares of \uc2 Boeing\uc2  fell about 2% on Tuesday. The stock has declined about 30% so far this year.\par \par \uc2 The end of the strike promises to bring some relief to \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's sprawling supply chain and airlines that rely on 737s, such as \uc2 Ryanair\uc2  and \uc2 Southwest Airlines\uc2 . Some have had to curb schedules, furlough workers or lower financial targets as they await delayed \uc2 Boeing\uc2  planes.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, fuselage maker \uc2 Spirit AeroSystems\uc2  warned in a regulatory filing about its ability to continue as a going concern. \uc2 Boeing\uc2  agreed in July to take over the money-losing supplier.\par \par \uc2 Restarting factories that build \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's bestselling 737 and other jets will take weeks, and the company has said that it would be months before \uc2 Boeing\uc2  reaches the production levels that it had previously hoped to reach by the end of this year.\par \par \uc2 "This contract was different, we had massive leverage this time," Jon Holden, president of the local chapter of \uc2 International Association of Machinists and Aerospace Workers\uc2  said in announcing the results. "This was a defining moment tonight. It's time for us to come together."\par \par \uc2 In the end, \uc2 Boeing\uc2  nearly matched the union's initial demand for a 40% wage increase. The increase was up from \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's original offer of 25%. After sweetening its wage proposal, \uc2 Boeing\uc2  said the average machinist's wage would reach about $119,000 at the end of the deal.\par \par \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's third offer got the support of 59% of the votes cast by union members. Holden said about 26,000 members voted for the deal, which also included a $12,000 ratification bonus and pledge to build the next plane in the Pacific Northwest. \uc2 Boeing\uc2  builds the widebody 787 at a nonunion factory in South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 Ortberg said that he is pleased with the result. "While the past few months have been difficult for all of us, we are all part of the same team," he said. "We will only move forward by listening and working together."\par \par \uc2 The White House praised the two sides for reaching a deal and said administration officials were involved in the process. "This contract is also important for \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's future as a critical part of America's aerospace sector," President Biden said.\par \par \uc2 November was set to be a tipping point for many of \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's suppliers. \uc2 Boeing\uc2  largely stopped taking delivery of new parts at the outset of the strike, which left parts makers with a decision: halt production and lay off workers moves that could stymie the industry's efforts to restart production when \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's machinists returned to work -- or continue production and absorb the cost of mounting inventory.\par \par \uc2 Other big suppliers were trying to avoid layoffs because it could take a year to rehire and train new workers -- but time was running out. The union's yes vote Monday means some of those suppliers will now avoid layoffs or stoppages.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020241106ekb60001c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
