\par\par \b \uc2 Finance & Markets: \uc2 KPMG\uc2  Lays Off 4% of Its U.S. Audit Workforce\b0\par\par\uc2 By Mark Maurer \par \uc2 385 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 November 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B10\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 KPMG\uc2  is laying off hundreds of employees in its U.S. auditing business as it works to make up for lower levels of voluntary turnover.\par \par \uc2 The Big Four accounting firm last week notified about 330 people, or nearly 4% of its roughly 9,000-person U.S. audit workforce, that they would be let go in the coming weeks, people familiar with the matter said. The cuts have focused on employees such as associates and managers, and included no partners, the people said.\par \par \uc2 The move follows several rounds of \uc2 KPMG\uc2  cuts in the U.S., including an audit-centric round in March. Last year, \uc2 KPMG\uc2  laid off about 5% of its U.S. staff over the summer, including advisory, tax and back-office workers, after cutting some advisory personnel, or almost 2% of U.S. staff, earlier in the year.\par \par \uc2 KPMG\uc2  is among the large accounting firms that have experienced slower-than-expected levels of voluntary attrition after aggressively hiring people during the pandemic. Layoffs at several firms have focused on the advisory side, for which revenue growth has generally slowed as corporate clients pull back on certain services, as opposed to audit.\par \par \uc2 KPMG\uc2 's global audit revenue grew 6% to $12.6 billion for the year ended Sept. 30, 2023, compared with 3% the previous year. The firm hasn't yet reported global revenue for its 2024 fiscal year.\par \par \uc2 The firm is expanding its U.S. audit business while sustaining quality, \uc2 KPMG\uc2  said. "The actions reflect our ongoing focus to align the size, shape and skills of our workforce to the market, while addressing continued low levels of attrition," the firm said in a statement, referring to the latest job cuts.\par \par \uc2 PwC\uc2 's U.S. unit recently laid off about 1,800 workers, some of which worked in audit, The Wall Street Journal reported.\par \par \uc2 Audit firms have struggled to find skilled accounting personnel, part of a deepening shortage in the profession. \uc2 KPMG\uc2 , which has said it isn't experiencing a shortage, last month supported allowing prospective accountants to bypass a fifth year of school to make the path to a state accounting license less expensive. Many states are exploring changes to certified public accountant licensing laws to provide an alternative.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020241105ekb50000k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
