\par\par \b \uc2 Boeing Union Votes To End Strike, Ratify Pact With 38% Raise\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sharon Terlep \par \uc2 663 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 November 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's machinists ratified a new labor deal, ending a paralyzing strike after nearly eight weeks and clearing the way for the plane maker to restart its factories.\par \par \uc2 Workers voted in favor of a contract that delivers a 38% wage increase over the life of the four-year deal for 33,000 machinists in the Pacific Northwest who build most of \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's jets.\par \par \uc2 The walkout has strained the finances of a critical American manufacturer that has been hobbled by quality problems and deep losses in its commercial and defense operations. Chief Executive \uc2 Kelly Ortberg\uc2  is cutting 17,000 jobs and raising more than $24 billion in equity to keep the company afloat.\par \par \uc2 The end of the strike promises to bring some relief to \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's sprawling supply chain and airlines that rely on 737s, such as \uc2 Ryanair\uc2  and \uc2 Southwest Airlines\uc2 . Some have had to curb schedules or curb financial targets as they await delayed \uc2 Boeing\uc2  planes.\par \par \uc2 The members of the \uc2 International Association of Machinists and Aerospace Workers\uc2  are set to begin returning to work on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Restarting factories that build \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's bestselling 737 and other jets will take weeks, and the company has said that it would be months before \uc2 Boeing\uc2  reaches the production levels that it had previously hoped to reach by the end of this year.\par \par \uc2 "This contract was different, we had massive leverage this time," Jon Holden, president of the union chapter said in announcing the vote results. "This was a defining moment tonight. It's time for us to come together."\par \par \uc2 After union members rejected two prior offers, \uc2 Boeing\uc2  nearly matched the union's initial demand for a 40% wage increase. The company's original offer was a 25% increase. \uc2 Boeing\uc2  said the average machinist's wage would reach about $119,000 at the end of the deal.\par \par \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's third offer got the support of 59% of the votes cast by union members. Holden said about 26,000 members cast votes for the deal, which also included a $12,000 ratification bonus and pledge to build the next plane in the Pacific Northwest. \uc2 Boeing\uc2  builds the wide-body 787 at a nonunion factory in South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 November was set to be a tipping point for many of \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's suppliers. \uc2 Boeing\uc2  largely stopped taking delivery of new parts at the outset of the strike, which left part makers with a decision: halt production and lay off workers -- a move that could stymie the industry's efforts to restart production when \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's machinists return to work -- or continue production and absorb the cost of mounting inventory.\par \par \uc2 Fuselage maker \uc2 Spirit AeroSystems\uc2  furloughed hundreds of workers. A U.K.-based part maker, Senior, announced job cuts in October, citing production problems at \uc2 Boeing\uc2  and its European rival, \uc2 Airbus\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Other big suppliers were trying to avoid layoffs because it could take a year to rehire and train new workers -- but time was running out. The union's yes vote Monday means some of those suppliers will now avoid layoffs or costly work stoppages.\par \par \uc2 Boeing\uc2  has warned investors that it expects to remain cash-flow negative in 2025 after burning through more than $10 billion in the first nine months of this year.\par \par \uc2 Before the strike, \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's output was well below its goals as the company slowed its factories to address quality issues that surfaced after a door-plug panel fell off a 737 MAX in midflight. \uc2 Boeing\uc2  has also struggled to hire and train enough workers after the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Ortberg, the CEO, said on a call with analysts in October that the relationship between the company and union must improve, "so we don't become so disconnected in the future."\par \par \uc2 Workers will return to sweeping quality-improvement and retraining efforts that Ortberg is working to implement in the wake of the door-plug blowout. \uc2 Boeing\uc2  is under pressure from airlines to ramp up deliveries.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020241105ekb50001x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
