\par\par \uc2 REVIEW & OUTLOOK (Editorial)\par \b \uc2 Ford Motor\uc2 's EV October Surprise\b0\par\par\uc2 432 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 November 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A12\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Talk about an October surprise for Michigan auto workers. \uc2 Ford Motor Co.\uc2  on Thursday said it will idle production of its F-150 Lightning electric truck in Dearborn from mid-November through the end of the year.\par \par \uc2 Ford's production stoppage follows a string of ominous announcements, starting in October 2023 when it announced plans to lay off about 700 workers who build the Lightning. In the spring Ford said it would eliminate two of three work crews at the Dearborn plant. In August it scrapped a planned electric SUV.\par \par \uc2 "We continue to adjust production for an optimal mix of sales growth and profitability," Ford said Thursday. As any business should. Ford's EV sales have lagged projections despite steep price cuts. Blame in part a market glut caused by government EV mandates.\par \par \uc2 Ford has sold a mere 22,807 Lightnings so far this year -- a far cry from the 180,000 the Dearborn factory was originally supposed to produce. The EV lobby and its friends in the press fawned over the Lightning when it debuted two years ago. The Lightning supposedly signaled that EVs had finally gone mainstream. Not quite.\par \par \uc2 Chief Financial Officer \uc2 John Lawler\uc2  this spring explained that battery advances are needed for mass adoption. "The bigger the vehicle, the bigger the battery. And the battery is the most expensive thing in the vehicle. And then the bigger the battery, the more weight. The more battery you need, the less efficient the vehicle is," he said. "So the costs just spiral out of control."\par \par \uc2 Ford's EV division has lost $3.7 billion during the first three quarters of this year -- about $55,000 per EV -- and expects $5 billion to $5.5 billion in losses this year. Under the \uc2 UAW\uc2  contract, workers earn a $1,000 bonus for every $1 billion in company profit. The EV losses could result in $5,000 in reduced pay for Ford workers.\par \par \uc2 They can thank \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  boss Shawn Fain for backing the Biden Administration's force-fed EV transition. \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  and GM have also reduced shifts and laid off thousands of workers who make internal-combustion engines. Yet Kamala Harris and Michigan Democratic Rep. \uc2 Elissa Slotkin\uc2 , who is running for Senate, keep saying EVs will be fantastic for auto workers.\par \par \uc2 One reason the election polls have narrowed in Michigan is that auto workers watching the EV transition from the front passenger seat don't believe them.\par \par \uc2 (See related letter: "Letters to the Editor: Auto Makers Put the Electric Vehicle Cart Before the Truck" -- WSJ Nov. 6, 2024)\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020241102ekb200021\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
