\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Meet the Headhunter-in-Chief for Trump --- \uc2 Howard Lutnick\uc2  is tasked with finding recruits for possible new administration\b0\par\par\uc2 By Rachel Louise Ensign \par \uc2 1,009 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 November 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Cantor Fitzgerald\uc2  Chief Executive \uc2 Howard Lutnick\uc2  hired thousands of employees after most of his company's New York staff was killed on Sept. 11, 2001. Now he is in charge of lining up candidates for a similar number of jobs in the event of a second Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 Lutnick, 63 years old, is MAGA's top emissary on Wall Street, a place where some executives have embraced former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's tax policies but are leery of the tone of his rallies. Trump, a longtime friend, appointed Lutnick co-chair of his transition team in August. His mandate is to compile shortlists of candidates for jobs ranging from attorney general to the deputy solicitor for water resources.\par \par \uc2 The billionaire says his experience running \uc2 Cantor\uc2  makes him uniquely suited for the role.\par \par \uc2 Terrorists flew a pair of passenger jets into the Twin Towers of the World Trade Center 23 years ago, killing all 658 Cantor employees who were in the office that morning. Lutnick, who wasn't at work because he was taking his son to his first day of kindergarten, made headlines as the CEO who wept on the news. He has spent the decades since rebuilding the business, finding success in niches such as crypto and the blank-check company craze of a few years ago.\par \par \uc2 Cantor Fitzgerald\uc2  now has 13,000 employees around the world, compared with about 2,000 after 9/11.\par \par \uc2 Now he has joined Trump's new inner circle of advisers from the business world. \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  is on board as the chief red-tape cutter. Investors John Paulson and Scott Bessent are dispensing economic advice and jockeying for the job of Treasury secretary.\par \par \uc2 Lutnick had a top speaking slot Sunday at Trump's Madison Square Garden rally. He is making the rounds on cable television to tout the former president's policies. On Wednesday, he questioned the safety of vaccines for children in an appearance on CNN.\par \par \uc2 He also tagged along with Trump to a campaign event in Michigan and a visit to the grave of an Orthodox Jewish leader.\par \par \uc2 Lutnick said in an interview in his Midtown Manhattan office that a second Trump administration would look different than the first. His team isn't interested in people such as former Defense Secretary \uc2 Jim Mattis\uc2  and former chief of staff \uc2 John Kelly\uc2 , who both worked in the first Trump administration, had their own ideas about how to do their jobs and eventually publicly condemned their former boss. Rather, Lutnick said, hires will "be loyal to the policies of the president."\par \par \uc2 Lutnick says he works for his companies from 6:30 a.m. to 9 a.m. and 4 p.m. to 10:30 p.m. In between, he volunteers for the Trump transition team. He is in back-to-back meetings with potential candidates and the executives and politicians vouching for them.\par \par \uc2 Recommendations come from executives including \uc2 Apollo Global Management\uc2  head \uc2 Marc Rowan\uc2  and brokerage founder \uc2 Charles Schwab\uc2 . In the middle of a meeting with a Journal reporter, a senator called.\par \par \uc2 Lutnick credits his rise at \uc2 Cantor\uc2  in part to wooing potential clients over drinks. And he doesn't shy away from a hard sell.\par \par \uc2 Years ago, he wanted to hire Alice Greenwald from the \uc2 U.S. Holocaust Memorial Museum\uc2  to be the director of National September 11 Memorial & Museum, where he sits on the board. When she declined, he told her having to watch someone else do the job was going to ruin her life.\par \par \uc2 He said she accepted the job a few days later.\par \par \uc2 Lutnick, a registered Republican, has donated to politicians in both parties over the years. In the 2016 election, he gave to Democratic presidential candidate \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  and to \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , who was running to represent California in the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 , according to OpenSecrets.org. He also donated to Republican presidential candidates Jeb Bush and Trump in that election cycle.\par \par \uc2 He first met Trump, whom he calls DJT, attending charity events in New York decades ago. More recently, the men played golf together. Lutnick and his wife, Allison, who have four children, gave to Trump's 2020 re-election bid.\par \par \uc2 "For my whole life I was a fiscal conservative, social liberal . . . The Democratic Party moved away from me," he said, citing the party's approach to illegal immigration as one example.\par \par \uc2 Trump called him about a year ago to ask him to help with his presidential bid. Lutnick hosted a $15 million fundraiser for the candidate in the Hamptons over the summer.\par \par \uc2 Musk took the stage with Lutnick at the \uc2 Madison Square Garden\uc2  rally. The pair of billionaires told the packed arena that they plan to create a Department of Government Efficiency, or DOGE, a cheeky nod to a cryptocurrency inspired by a Shiba Inu.\par \par \uc2 "How much do you think we can rip out of this wasted, $6.5 trillion Harris-Biden budget?" Lutnick asked Musk.\par \par \uc2 "I think we could do at least $2 trillion," Musk said.\par \par \uc2 "Yeah!" Lutnick yelled, pumping his fist. The crowd cheered.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Cantor Fitzgerald\uc2 's Businesses\par \par \uc2 Howard Lutnick\uc2  owns the majority of privately held \uc2 Cantor Fitzgerald\uc2 , which controls three companies, two of which are publicly traded.\par \par \uc2 BGC Group\uc2  is the old brokerage business, which arranges trades of bonds and other securities for the biggest banks. That company is behind an effort to dethrone the exchange giant \uc2 CME Group\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Newmark Group\uc2  is a commercial real-estate broker. And a third company called \uc2 Cantor\uc2  offers investment-banking services.\par \par \uc2 Cantor Fitzgerald\uc2  has ventured into some controversial corners of finance. A foray into sports betting led to a more than $22 million penalty for illegal gambling and money laundering. The unit was sold off in 2020.\par \par \uc2 It also has embraced crypto, which many larger companies avoid. Last year, the Journal reported that the secretive Hong Kong-based owner of the stablecoin tether used \uc2 Cantor Fitzgerald\uc2  to help oversee its $39 billion bond portfolio.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020241101ekb100010\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
