\par\par \uc2 The Property Report\par \b \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  to Staff: Work in Office or Else --- Coffee chain boosts its enforcement of its mandate for three days a week\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jennifer Calfas and Heather Haddon \par \uc2 365 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 October 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B6\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  is stepping up efforts to enforce its return-to-office mandate with a warning: Comply with the policy or risk termination.\par \par \uc2 The company will be instituting an "accountability process" in January to ensure corporate employees comply with requirements to work in the office three days a week, according to an internal message obtained by The Wall Street Journal. The message notes employees may face termination if they don't meet in-office requirements.\par \par \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2  reported the news Monday. \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  said the company's expectations for hybrid corporate employees haven't changed, but it is reminding workers they must follow them.\par \par \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  isn't increasing the number of days employees are back in the office.\par \par \uc2 "We are continuing to support our leaders as they hold their teams accountable," a spokesman said.\par \par \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 's new chief executive, \uc2 Brian Niccol\uc2 , has a hybrid work arrangement himself. The former CEO of \uc2 Chipotle Mexican Grill\uc2 , who took the helm of \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  in September, Niccol maintains a home in Southern California and commutes to the Seattle office by corporate jet.\par \par \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  has said Niccol would meet or exceed \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 's in-office mandates. Some workers have expressed skepticism of Niccol's arrangement.\par \par \uc2 For about two years, the company has required corporate employees to come to the office three days a week. The message sent this week said the company also will no longer be requiring employees in the office on Tuesday -- leaving individual leaders to determine the best common day for the teams.\par \par \uc2 The move comes as a flurry of companies reassess their in-office requirements years after the coronavirus pandemic prompted shutdowns and normalized remote work. Executives have said they seek to rebuild an office culture that existed before the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 A higher percentage of companies required employees to be in the office five days a week in the third quarter compared with the second quarter, according to Flex Index, which tracks workplace strategies. Meanwhile, U.S. office vacancy rates are stabilizing and investors are showing interest in office properties.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020241030ekau0000n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
