\par\par \uc2 REVIEW & OUTLOOK (Editorial)\par \b \uc2 Musk Beats Biden on Free Speech\b0\par\par\uc2 407 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 October 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A16\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  scored a First Amendment victory on Friday when the Fifth Circuit Court of Appeals ruled that the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2  can't take away his speech rights (\uc2 Tesla\uc2  v. \uc2 NLRB\uc2 ).\par \par \uc2 The Fifth Circuit's 9-8 en banc decision reverses a ruling that the \uc2 NLRB\uc2  could force Mr. Musk to delete a 2018 tweet that the \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  claimed was coercive. The \uc2 National Labor Relations\uc2  Act makes it an unfair labor practice for an employer "to interfere with, restrain, or coerce employees in the exercise" of their labor rights.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 NLRB\uc2  argues that Mr. Musk interfered with the United Auto Worker's efforts to organize a \uc2 Tesla\uc2  factory, citing a years-old tweet that responded to a \uc2 Twitter\uc2  user's question about his views on unions: "Nothing stopping \uc2 Tesla\uc2  team at our car plant from voting union. Could do so tmrw if they wanted. But why pay union dues & give up stock options for nothing?"\par \par \uc2 Mr. Musk later said the "\uc2 UAW\uc2  does not have individual stock ownership as part of the compensation at any other company." The \uc2 NLRB\uc2  ruled that Mr. Musk's statements were an unfair labor practice and ordered him to remove them. A split Fifth Circuit disagreed, holding that the \uc2 Tesla\uc2  CEO was entitled to express his views.\par \par \uc2 The board is "powerless to delete protected speech," the unsigned opinion says. "Deletion is a remedy for communications that are, in the First Amendment's contemplation, not speech at all," such as "obscene material, perjurious material, or other non-speech material." The majority declined to decide whether Mr. Musk's tweet violated labor law.\par \par \uc2 That's a shame because government chills speech rights when it punishes people for exercising them. Federal labor law says the "expressing of any views, argument, or opinion, or the dissemination" is not an unfair labor practice "if such expression contains no threat of reprisal or force or promise of benefit."\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court's Gissel (1969) precedent sets out a confusing standard for what employers can and cannot tell employees: "An employer is free only to tell 'what he reasonably believes will be the likely economic consequences of unionization that are outside his control,' and not 'threats of economic reprisal to be taken solely on his own volition.'"\par \par \uc2 It may not be long before the High Court is asked to weigh in on this collision between labor law and speech rights.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020241028ekas0000p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
