\par\par \b \uc2 Netflix\uc2  Pulls Plug on Videogame --- Streamer halts effort to create 'triple-A' title early in development\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sarah E. Needleman and Jessica Toonkel \par \uc2 591 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 October 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Netflix\uc2  is giving up on making the videogame equivalent of a Hollywood blockbuster and will instead focus on producing casual games going forward.\par \par \uc2 The streaming giant earlier this month stopped developing its own high-end game -- what the industry calls a Triple-A title -- less than a year into development, a spokeswoman confirmed. The company laid off a team of about 30 people in Southern California, including high-profile veterans who worked on franchises such as \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 's Halo and \uc2 Sony\uc2 's "God of War." The project was a multiplayer shooter game, according to a person familiar with the matter.\par \par \uc2 Triple-A games are typically made for consoles and gaming computers and can cost hundreds of millions of dollars to develop due to their size, complexity and cinematic quality. They are also increasingly being created for smartphones and tablets, and the one that \uc2 Netflix\uc2  was developing was intended to be played on a smartphone, tablet, TV or personal computer.\par \par \uc2 Netflix\uc2  has been working on a new app, "Netflix Game Controller," that can turn a smartphone phone into a controller so that games can be played on a TV or computer. The company is testing the app in more than a half-dozen countries, including the U.S., Germany and Mexico.\par \par \uc2 Netflix\uc2  could still put Triple-A games from third parties on its platform. Earlier this year, the company added three such titles from \uc2 Take-Two Interactive Software\uc2 's "Grand Theft Auto" series, though they debuted in the early 2000s.\par \par \uc2 The pivot comes after \uc2 Netflix\uc2  in June replaced its head of gaming, \uc2 Mike Verdu\uc2 , with \uc2 Alain Tascan\uc2 , who was previously executive vice president of development at "Fortnite" maker \uc2 Epic Games\uc2 . Verdu has since taken on a new role at \uc2 Netflix\uc2  involving tech and innovation for games.\par \par \uc2 Independent games journalist Stephen Totilo earlier reported on the cancellation of the Triple-A game under development and the dismissal of the team behind it.\par \par \uc2 Last week, \uc2 Netflix\uc2  reported its most profitable quarter ever despite slowing subscriber growth. The company added 5.07 million subscribers in the third quarter, compared with 8.76 million net new subscribers during the same period a year earlier.\par \par \uc2 Netflix\uc2  moved into videogames in 2021, part of a strategy to keep users coming back to the streaming service when their favorite shows are between seasons and to attract new subscribers. The company, which has also pushed into advertising to diversify revenue, bought multiple game studios to create original titles and license others from third parties. But building up the business has been challenging.\par \par \uc2 Netflix\uc2  has launched more than 100 games to date, mostly casual and designed for playing on mobile devices, such as "Oxenfree" and "Too Hot to Handle."\par \par \uc2 Growth in game downloads has slowed since late 2023, and about 1% of total \uc2 Netflix\uc2  daily active users play its games, according to analytics firm \uc2 Apptopia\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The videogame industry is fiercely competitive and dominated by a handful of legacy businesses such as "Madden NFL" maker \uc2 Electronic Arts\uc2  and "Call of Duty" developer \uc2 Activision Blizzard\uc2 . Since 2022, consumer spending on game content of all kinds has barely grown, and game companies have laid off tens of thousands of workers.\par \par \uc2 This year, \uc2 Netflix\uc2  has been considering ways to make money from its games, The Wall Street Journal previously reported. Those could include offering in-app purchases, charging for more sophisticated games or giving some subscribers access to games with ads in them.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020241023ekan0003u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
