\par\par \uc2 REVIEW & OUTLOOK (Editorial)\par \b \uc2 Trump, Harris and \uc2 McDonald's\b0\par\par\uc2 714 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 October 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A16\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  on Sunday visited a \uc2 McDonald\uc2 's franchise in Pennsylvania where he strapped on an apron and served french fries to drive-through customers. It was a nice photo-op. But the fast-food aficionado missed a bigger opportunity to highlight how \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 's agenda endangers such jobs and franchise restaurants.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Harris says she once worked at a McDonald's, while Mr. Trump says she hasn't. We don't know who's right. But there's no doubt that fast-food jobs provide young people lessons in treating customers courteously, showing up on time, and taking and following orders, which can serve them well throughout their careers. So why does the Vice President want to make it harder to work there?\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Start with the Biden National Labor Relations Board's joint-employer rule, which seeks to hold corporate parents responsible for the labor practices of franchisees. The agency's goal is to make it easier for unions to organize workers at franchises. The rule would reduce franchisee autonomy and increase their costs, resulting in fewer jobs.\par \par \uc2 The Senate and House this spring approved bipartisan resolutions to block the rule. Voting to keep the rule were Democratic Sens. Bob Casey (Pa.), \uc2 Tammy Baldwin\uc2  (Wis.), \uc2 Sherrod Brown\uc2  (Ohio), \uc2 Jacky Rosen\uc2  (Nevada.) and Michigan Rep. \uc2 Elissa Slotkin\uc2 . President Biden vetoed the resolution. The Pro Act, which Ms. Harris supports, would codify the rule.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Harris has also backed raising the federal minimum wage from $7.25 an hour, though she hasn't specified a level. The Administration has endorsed a $15 federal minimum, and some on the left want to increase it to $20. This is a recipe for more jobless teens.\par \par \uc2 A handful of states have increased their minimum wage to $15 or more. A recent Beacon Economics study found that median unemployment was 3.1% in the 20 states that mimic the federal minimum wage, versus 4.2% in the 15 states with minimums between $14 and $17.\par \par \uc2 Higher minimum wages price teens out of the job market since they usually have the least experience and skills. Look no further than Ms. Harris's home state of California, where Democrats raised the minimum to $16 an hour from $12 in 2020. Over the past two years, unemployment among those ages 16 to 19 has soared to 19.2% from 10.8%, versus 11.9% from 10.5% nationwide. California's new $20 minimum wage for fast-food workers has compounded the problem.\par \par \uc2 Like other restaurant chains, \uc2 McDonald\uc2 's has had to cope with a tight labor market and inflationary costs. This has contributed to higher prices. The average cost of a Big Mac meal nationwide has increased to $9.29 from $7.29 in 2019. A Hamburger Happy Meal has risen to $4.69 from $3.00.\par \par \uc2 Progressives accuse McDonald's of "price gouging," but its prices have increased in line with its cost of goods and labor. Yet Ms. Harris wants to give the \uc2 Federal Trade Commission\uc2  authority to punish alleged price gouging by the food industry -- that is, fix prices. \uc2 FTC\uc2  Chair Lina Khan's agency would determine if \uc2 McDonald\uc2 's franchisees are charging too much for a happy meal.\par \par \uc2 Relatedly, the \uc2 FTC\uc2  last year sought public comment on franchise agreements including "the pervasiveness and rationale for franchisors marketing their franchises using languages other than English." Ms. Khan apparently doesn't like that \uc2 McDonald\uc2 's and other corporations are giving minorities opportunities to launch businesses.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FTC\uc2  wants to micromanage franchise relationships under the guise of protecting workers and franchisees. Ms. Khan is also seeking to collect information that she and her union friends can use to sue franchised businesses. The \uc2 Service Employees International Union\uc2  (\uc2 SEIU\uc2 ) says as much.\par \par \uc2 The franchise system has "legalized the use of myriad vertical restraints that allow franchisors" to avoid "the costs and liability that legal responsibility for those small businesses and their workers would entail," the \uc2 SEIU\uc2  wrote in a public comment. For this reason, franchise practices "may also be vulnerable to \uc2 FTC\uc2  challenge as unfair methods of competition."\par \par \uc2 Ms. Harris talks up her grants and loans for small businesses, but the local \uc2 McDonald\uc2 's and other franchisees are on her target list. That won't help low-income workers.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020241022ekam0000p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
