\par\par \b \uc2 Business News: Seasonal Logistics Hiring Holds Steady --- Expectations of a muted holiday rush keep staffing plans even with last year\b0\par\par\uc2 By Liz Young \par \uc2 687 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 October 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Logistics operators and fulfillment providers are largely holding their seasonal hiring plans steady with last year as they prepare for what they expect to be a muted holiday shopping season.\par \par \uc2 Amazon.com\uc2  plans to hire about 250,000 workers for the peak season for a second straight year. Target said it will bring on 100,000 additional workers this year across its stores and distribution centers, the same as last year. \uc2 Walmart\uc2  didn't disclose an exact number of planned hires, but said its seasonal staffing will be consistent with recent years.\par \par \uc2 United Parcel Service\uc2  is an exception to the sector's overall caution, with plans to hire 125,000 workers this year, up from 100,000 seasonal hires in 2023. The delivery giant said it is hiring more people to accommodate a shorter period this year between Black Friday, the day after Thanksgiving that generally marks the start of the holiday shopping rush, and Christmas compared with last year.\par \par \uc2 "With four fewer operating days during our peak holiday season, we've increased hiring so that we can be even more confident we will meet anticipated customer needs," a \uc2 UPS\uc2  spokesperson said.\par \par \uc2 UPS\uc2 's labor contract, struck last year -- a five-year, $30 billion pact with the \uc2 International Brotherhood of Teamsters\uc2  that covers roughly 330,000 package-delivery drivers and package sorters -- includes stricter limits on overtime.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 U.S. Postal Service\uc2  said it will hire about 7,500 workers, down from 10,000 last year. The agency said it is already well-staffed after making a hiring push over the past four years.\par \par \uc2 FedEx\uc2  hasn't disclosed its seasonal hiring plans. The parcel carrier has been working on combining its Ground and Express networks in the U.S., which could affect its seasonal employment plans.\par \par \uc2 Companies are setting their seasonal hiring in an uncertain retail economy. Retail trade has been volatile this year, with the most-recent government figures showing sales edging up 0.1% in August.\par \par \uc2 Consulting firm \uc2 Deloitte\uc2  projects holiday spending this year will grow between 2.3% and 3.3% from last year, below the 4.3% year-over-year expansion in 2023 and far below the 7.6% year-over-year growth in 2022.\par \par \uc2 Some companies also are dialing back hiring to fit what they believe will be a longer, flatter period of demand this year rather than a sharp spike leading up to Christmas. \uc2 Amazon\uc2 , Target and \uc2 Walmart\uc2  each held big sales earlier this month, kicking off the shopping season over a month before Black Friday. About half of shoppers recently surveyed by financial-services company \uc2 Bankrate\uc2  said they planned to start buying gifts before Halloween.\par \par \uc2 Billy Peterson, senior vice president of service-delivery operations at e-commerce fulfillment provider \uc2 Radial\uc2 , said the company plans to hire about 7,000 seasonal workers this year, down from 9,500 last year. Workers will start in phases as they are needed rather than in a single big hiring push as in years past.\par \par \uc2 "There may be multiple start dates even within a site that would be closer to the time frames that the people would actually be required," Peterson said.\par \par \uc2 Logistics payrolls were down by 22,300 jobs in September from the previous year to about 4.4 million positions, and well below the peak of nearly 4.7 million workers in June 2022, according to seasonally-adjusted preliminary figures from the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Satish Jindel\uc2 , president of \uc2 ShipMatrix\uc2 , which analyzes parcel-shipping data, said he expects parcel carriers will be cautious with their seasonal hiring plans amid a longer shopping season and as more shoppers return to in-person stores rather than placing orders online.\par \par \uc2 ShipMatrix\uc2  estimates that carriers will deliver 106 million parcels a day during the peak holiday season that starts around Thanksgiving and ends in January. Last year, the industry delivered an average of roughly 83 million to 84 million parcels a day during that period, \uc2 ShipMatrix\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Esther Fung contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020241021ekal00032\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
