\par\par \uc2 REVIEW & OUTLOOK (Editorial)\par \b \uc2 Whitmer's Home-Care Cash Grab\b0\par\par\uc2 534 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 October 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A16\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 More Americans are working to help an aging parent, and Michigan Gov. \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  wants a piece of the action. She thinks these family caregivers are public employees, ripe for dues collection, and she wants her union friends to cash in.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Whitmer signed a bill last week that classifies "individual home health caregivers" as state employees -- but only for the purpose of collective bargaining. People who prepare meals or measure out pills for a relative at home won't get access to typical government perks, like gold-plated health insurance. Their "benefit" will be getting nudged to join a new branch of the \uc2 Service Employees International Union\uc2  (\uc2 SEIU\uc2 ).\par \par \uc2 If enough caregivers vote to adopt the union, the \uc2 SEIU\uc2  will claim dues from the payments that its new members receive from Home Help, a Medicaid program in Michigan that offers $13.53 an hour to family caregivers. Each member who joins would owe the union about 2.75% of the stipend. The union stands to collect up to $13 million a year, according to the Mackinac Center for Public Policy.\par \par \uc2 But caregivers can't expect bigger stipends in return for those dues. Their union will bargain with Michigan's health department, which has no power to boost Home Help funding without legislative approval. The \uc2 SEIU\uc2  will take cash from struggling families and repay them with little more than moral support.\par \par \uc2 The scheme is so bald that Michigan has already rejected it twice. Family caregivers were first deemed public employees 20 years ago under Democratic Gov. \uc2 Jennifer Granholm\uc2 , and the \uc2 SEIU\uc2  skimmed $34 million out of home care stipends from 2006 to 2012. Republicans in the Legislature let the program lapse, but the union launched a ballot measure to restore it, certain that the public would support union rights for care workers. The measure failed 56% to 44%.\par \par \uc2 Democrats won't let the idea die because the \uc2 SEIU\uc2  is among their biggest funders, contributing more than $200 million to the Harris-Walz campaign and other races this election cycle. Gov. Whitmer says restoring the union will boost care workers' stipends, leaving out that the state doubled Home Help funding between 2014 and this year, while the workers had no bargaining rights. The \uc2 SEIU\uc2  and Democrats are the only winners in the stipend skim.\par \par \uc2 That's why lawmakers want to make sure caregivers don't even think about not joining the \uc2 SEIU\uc2 . The new law requires all care workers to attend "orientation" sessions, where they'll supposedly learn the mechanics of taking care of their loved ones -- i.e., the work they are already doing.\par \par \uc2 The real purpose is to make them sit through a 30-minute pitch from the \uc2 SEIU\uc2  about the benefits of joining up. Republicans proposed an amendment that would have required the state to inform workers of their right not to join, but Democrats voted it down.\par \par \uc2 Several other states have reclassified home-care workers as public employees, including California, Oregon and Washington, giving unions a key into ordinary households. Progressive lawmakers are happy to count anyone who receives state payments as a de facto government worker, and to tap another funding source for union allies.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020241017ekah0003h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
