\par\par \b \uc2 McKinsey\uc2  Cuts China Workforce as It Seeks To Ease Security Risk\b0\par\par\uc2 By Yoko Kubota in Beijing and Raffaele Huang n Singapore \par \uc2 955 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 October 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 McKinsey\uc2  is overhauling its China business after cutting back on government-linked clients and reducing the unit's workforce by nearly 500 people, about a third of the total.\par \par \uc2 To reduce security risks associated with doing business in China, the U.S.-based consulting firm has been separating its China operations from other global operations, people familiar with the matter said -- a practice increasingly popular among multinational companies.\par \par \uc2 Western businesses with decades of experience in China -- both \uc2 McKinsey\uc2  and those it advises -- are facing some of their most challenging times because of geopolitical tensions and the slowing Chinese economy. \uc2 McKinsey\uc2  has been facing scrutiny in Washington over work linked to the Chinese government, while some Chinese clients have been shifting to local rivals.\par \par \uc2 Over the past two years, \uc2 McKinsey\uc2 's workforce in Greater China, which includes Hong Kong and Taiwan, has shrunk by hundreds of employees, people familiar with the matter said. \uc2 McKinsey\uc2  in June 2023 referred to having nearly 1,500 employees in the region on its Greater China website. Joe Ngai, the head of \uc2 McKinsey\uc2 's China business, said the company's employee-attrition rate in the country has been at its historic average of around 20%, but it has slowed hiring. \uc2 McKinsey\uc2  still has more than 1,000 employees in Greater China, he said.\par \par \uc2 It has stopped working with Chinese local-government clients and cut back on state-linked projects, which used to account for a significant portion of its projects. Its latest strategy calls for helping multinational companies navigate China's changes, advising Chinese companies that want to expand overseas and working with Chinese companies going through executive transitions, said Ngai.\par \par \uc2 "Our clients all face challenges. We have to help them face up to these challenges," said Ngai in an interview. In a difficult and slow-growth market, "many of \uc2 McKinsey\uc2 's core skills and capabilities are actually more important than ever," he said.\par \par \uc2 McKinsey\uc2 's reshaping of its China business comes as rival consulting firms continue to undertake extensive work for government agencies and state-controlled companies.\par \par \uc2 McKinsey\uc2  first opened in mainland China in 1993 and grew quickly alongside the nation's economy, with both Chinese and multinational clients. \uc2 Dominic Barton\uc2 , global managing partner from 2009 to 2018, had earlier worked in Shanghai, and his successor, \uc2 Kevin Sneader\uc2 , had worked in Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2 McKinsey\uc2  was known for winning business from China's biggest state-owned firms, which play a central role in the economy. These have included China Construction Bank and China Telecom, which \uc2 McKinsey\uc2  advised on digitization and operation management projects. Another big client was privately owned insurer Ping An.\par \par \uc2 Ngai told Chinese news website Sohu Finance in 2020 that about 80% of the clients in \uc2 McKinsey\uc2 's China business at the time were domestic companies, with half of those being state-owned firms. The remaining 20% were multinational firms, he said. Ngai and a \uc2 McKinsey\uc2  representative didn't give a client breakdown for past years or today.\par \par \uc2 While consulting fees in China tend to be lower than in the U.S., an issue that led to some tension within \uc2 McKinsey\uc2 , the firm generally was willing to overlook it in pursuit of growth, said people familiar with its strategy.\par \par \uc2 That strategy has changed.\par \par \uc2 In Washington, American lawmakers have been criticizing \uc2 McKinsey\uc2  for advising the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  -- including on defense-related projects -- while also consulting for the Chinese government or affiliated organizations. Its global managing partner, \uc2 Bob Sternfels\uc2 , was grilled in Congress about the subject this year.\par \par \uc2 In the wake of the scrutiny over conflicts of interest, \uc2 McKinsey\uc2  has been revising its client list and cutting back on certain projects in China. A \uc2 McKinsey\uc2  representative said that the central government of China isn't, and to its knowledge has never been, the company's client.\par \par \uc2 Asked about \uc2 McKinsey\uc2 's current work with Chinese state-owned companies, Ngai said \uc2 McKinsey\uc2  selects clients rigorously and follows protocols to prevent conflicts of interest.\par \par \uc2 Within China, the biggest challenges include economic sluggishness and Beijing's clampdown on the business of due diligence and information-gathering by foreign consultancies. Last year, police visited Bain's Shanghai office and separately raided the offices of Capvision, a consulting firm that connects investors with individual experts who provide industry intelligence.\par \par \uc2 McKinsey\uc2  also faces price competition. Some Chinese companies in the market for advice are turning to less-expensive domestic alternatives to U.S. brands.\par \par \uc2 Companies with names little-known abroad -- among them Allpku Management Consulting and StratOp Group -- are picking up clients, as are local technology giants such as \uc2 Alibaba Group\uc2  and \uc2 Huawei Technologies\uc2  for projects involving digitization.\par \par \uc2 Last year, foreign consulting firms' revenue in China fell 6.3% while local consultants increased revenue slightly, according to Chinese consulting market-research firm Shensixing. The foreign firms held a 40% share of the $7.7 billion market, Shensixing said.\par \par \uc2 McKinsey\uc2 , whose global revenue was around $16 billion in 2023, doesn't disclose its revenue or profitability in China. Globally, the company has about 45,000 employees.\par \par \uc2 This summer, some of \uc2 McKinsey\uc2 's senior consultants gathered in Singapore, where Ngai briefed them about \uc2 McKinsey\uc2 's China strategy running through 2026, people familiar with the event said.\par \par \uc2 Among its work with Chinese businesses seeking to go overseas, \uc2 McKinsey\uc2  has advised on projects aimed at selling Chinese electric vehicles overseas, people familiar with the matter said.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, \uc2 McKinsey\uc2  has taken some steps to separate its China operations from the rest of the world. Its global computer systems are being divided, with mainland China employees set to be cut off from accessing certain databases and documents, people familiar with the matter said.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020241017ekah0000k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
