\par\par \b \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  to Add Mexican Output --- Move to build more Rams in country comes amid union criticism of job cuts\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ben Glickman \par \uc2 885 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 October 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Ram's parent company is taking steps to build its bestselling truck in Mexico, a move that threatens to inflame the automaker's frayed relationship with its workers union.\par \par \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , which also houses \uc2 Jeep\uc2 , Dodge and Chrysler brands, is expanding its factory complex in northern Mexico to build Ram 1500 pickup trucks, according to people familiar with the matter. The company in recent years has made nearly all of its light-duty Ram pickups at a factory near Detroit.\par \par \uc2 The company said Tuesday that the Ram trucks would continue to be made at its Sterling Heights assembly plant north of Detroit, and that "no other announcements have been made about production of the Ram 1500."\par \par \uc2 The automaker also noted that it recently disclosed plans to invest $235 million at the Michigan factory to build future electric versions of the pickup. "The investment will bring innovations to the plant to support a multienergy approach that is laser-focused on meeting customer demand," it said.\par \par \uc2 The Mexico plans come about a year after \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  signed a landmark labor agreement with the \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2 , granting union members a substantial pay hike and promising billions in U.S.-based investment. Analysts have said the contract, which the union called the richest in its history, would increase labor costs for \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  and its rivals and could compel the companies to shift some factory work outside of the country.\par \par \uc2 A \uc2 UAW\uc2  spokeswoman didn't reply to a request for comment on the company's Mexico plans.\par \par \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  has been building additional factory space next to its existing plant in Saltillo, Mexico, the people said. Satellite images reviewed by The Wall Street Journal show that two roughly half-kilometer-long buildings have been constructed in the past several months.\par \par \uc2 The plans to produce Ram pickups in the space could still change, the people said.\par \par \uc2 It couldn't be learned whether the planned factory space in Saltillo would be used to make battery- or gasoline-powered versions of Ram trucks. Electric Ram trucks are set to hit dealer lots before the end of the year, among other new battery-powered offerings.\par \par \uc2 Former President Donald Trump has criticized U.S.-based automakers for producing vehicles in Mexico. Locked in a tight race with Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  in the swing state of Michigan, Trump has promised to save American auto manufacturing jobs with harsh penalties on companies making cars south of the border.\par \par \uc2 Trump in a recent visit to Detroit, the historic center of the American auto industry, threatened tariffs of 100% or more on all vehicles made in Mexico.\par \par \uc2 The expansion around the northern Mexico city of Saltillo risks ratcheting up tensions with the \uc2 UAW\uc2  and its president, Shawn Fain. The union in recent weeks threatened to strike at \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 's U.S. plants over planned factory investments that Fain claims the company is slow-walking. The automaker has denied this characterization, arguing it has flexibility in the timing of its plans.\par \par \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  Chief Executive \uc2 Carlos Tavares\uc2  has been under pressure to reverse a steep slide in U.S. market share this year, which in part triggered a recent profit warning. Shares have fallen 45% this year, among the worst performing automotive stocks.\par \par \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  has been making vehicles in Saltillo since the mid-1990s, including a heavy-duty version of the Ram pickup and some 1,500 trucks in past years. At a separate plant in the area, the company makes Ram ProMaster vans.\par \par \uc2 Ram trucks are \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 's bestselling vehicles in the U.S. and have been a major contributor to the trans-Atlantic carmaker's surging profits in years past.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 UAW\uc2  has pushed \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  and other carmakers to safeguard manufacturing jobs in the U.S. As part of a historic contract with the \uc2 UAW\uc2 , \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  agreed to max out its truck-building capacity in Sterling Heights before making Ram pickups in Mexico -- meaning the additions in Mexico aren't meant to result in a decline at the U.S. factory.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 UAW\uc2  has raised concerns that the company would move Ram pickup-truck production out of the U.S. at the expense of union workers.\par \par \uc2 "There's fear across the board," said Kevin Gotinsky, \uc2 UAW\uc2 's lead bargainer for \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , at a union rally in suburban Detroit last week. "The shift is everything out of this country."\par \par \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  said in a statement that it would continue to abide by last year's union contract as it manages the transition to electric vehicles.\par \par \uc2 Automakers have shifted manufacturing to Mexico for decades in a push to lower costs, helped by free-trade agreements established between Mexico and the U.S. \uc2 Tesla\uc2  more recently has said it would invest in a new facility in the country, though some manufacturers' plans have been put on pause until after the U.S. election.\par \par \uc2 Some Chinese car manufacturers have also set their sights on production in Mexico, which could allow them to circumvent tariffs on future shipments to the U.S.\par \par \uc2 Stellantis in recent years has grown increasingly reliant on Mexican facilities to make vehicles sold in North America.\par \par \uc2 About 27% of \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  vehicles made in the region this year were built in Mexico.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020241016ekag00014\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
