\par\par \b \uc2 Unions Defy Their Leadership With 'No' Votes on Contracts\b0\par\par\uc2 By Lauren Weber and Sharon Terlep \par \uc2 1,004 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 October 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Frustrated by inflation eating into their paychecks and empowered by the \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2 ' successful strikes last year, union members are sending a message to their own leaders: Do better.\par \par \uc2 In recent months, members have voted down contracts at \uc2 Boeing\uc2 , \uc2 AT&T\uc2 , \uc2 Textron\uc2  and other companies after their unions' negotiating committees spent months hammering out deals for them to ratify.\par \par \uc2 Most dramatically, at \uc2 Boeing\uc2 , 94% of machinists in the company's largest union voted in September against the proposed contract, which offered 25% wage increases over four years. Members of the \uc2 International Association of Machinists and Aerospace Workers\uc2  chapter had never before rejected a contract recommended by their leaders.\par \par \uc2 The IAM chapter president had described the deal as a historic raise. "I would like to imagine he did everything he could to secure the best possible deal for his members," said Josh McKenzie, who works at \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's 737 factory in Renton, Wash. "At face value, it didn't look bad. But the more I read it, the more it seemed like we'd be in pretty much the same boat."\par \par \uc2 The typical U.S. union member still has higher earnings than workers who aren't covered by a union. But the gap has narrowed in recent years as many unionized workers were locked into multiyear collective bargaining contracts that had been signed before the postpandemic inflation surge.\par \par \uc2 Cathy Creighton, a labor lawyer at Cornell University's ILR School, said union leaders are running into extra resistance this year because the workforce is riled up.\par \par \uc2 "Employees are mad because they don't have their fair share of the pie, and they want to go on strike," she said.\par \par \uc2 That sentiment helped propel a swath of dockworkers to strike earlier this month, an action that ended only when port employers promised a higher pay increase than the one in their initial proposal. With a 62% raise in hand, the two sides are now positioned to negotiate over automation issues.\par \par \uc2 Workers are encouraged by a relatively tight labor market and by the \uc2 UAW\uc2 's success securing a new contract last year with \uc2 General Motors\uc2 , \uc2 Ford Motor\uc2  and Chrysler parent \uc2 Stellantis\uc2 , following a bold strategy of strikes at multiple locations.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 UAW\uc2  negotiated a 25% wage bump over four years, along with a shorter time period to reach top pay, improved retirement benefits and the right to strike over plant closures. Even then, some of the votes to get to victory were nail-bitingly close.\par \par \uc2 If the \uc2 UAW\uc2 's negotiations "had ended in a dismal failure, I think things would be different," Creighton said.\par \par \uc2 Last month, some 5,000 workers at \uc2 Textron\uc2 's Wichita, Kan.-based aviation unit, also represented by the IAM, voted overwhelmingly to reject a contract recommended by union leaders.\par \par \uc2 They have been on strike since Sept. 23 and have yet to resume negotiations. The deal called for a 26% pay increase over four years.\par \par \uc2 "This is a culmination of all workers realizing that they have lost ground," said Brian Bryant, international president of the \uc2 IAM union\uc2 , with 600,000 members. "This is a continuation of what you saw with the \uc2 UAW\uc2  -- 'enough is enough.'"\par \par \uc2 When companies are negotiating multiple deals at once, union members take note. About 8,000 \uc2 AT&T\uc2  employees in California and Nevada represented by the \uc2 Communications Workers of America\uc2  union had worked without a contract since April. When the group's bargaining committee offered a new contract proposal in September, members rejected it.\par \par \uc2 By then, the western workers had caught a tailwind: Their peers in several southeastern states were on strike over their own bargaining process. The telecom giant later reached tentative agreements with both groups. The two proposed contracts, including a sweetened agreement for employees in the West, are scheduled to tally votes on Oct. 18.\par \par \uc2 Sometimes leaders have strategic reasons for putting forward a deal they suspect their members will reject, particularly if the company is refusing to negotiate better terms. A "no" vote can embarrass the company and set off strikes.\par \par \uc2 In the case of the \uc2 Boeing\uc2  strike, the union leadership told members in a memo before the vote that they had "achieved the best contract we have ever had."\par \par \uc2 Members reacted with outrage in online forums and on the shop floor, calling the pay raise paltry and criticizing the company's move to do away with annual bonuses. Many were skeptical of a next-generation plane commitment. Some also wanted \uc2 Boeing\uc2  to restore pension benefits.\par \par \uc2 Jon Holden, president of the local \uc2 Boeing\uc2  machinists chapter, said he believes the union leaders needed to recommend the deal to get the company to agree to a 25% raise and other improvements.\par \par \uc2 "We had to recommend it or we would have been at 18% with no job security," he said. "We negotiated the best contract we could short of a strike."\par \par \uc2 Talks between the two sides stalled this past week. In a legal filing, \uc2 Boeing\uc2  accused union leaders of undermining the deal they struck. The union accused \uc2 Boeing\uc2  of sidestepping its leadership.\par \par \uc2 It often takes a few tries to reach a deal in the airline industry. Bargaining can stretch on for years, with government rules that make it difficult to strike even after union members reject contracts.\par \par \uc2 Alaska Airlines\uc2 ' flight attendants voted down a tentative agreement in August, which offered an average raise of 32% over three years.\par \par \uc2 Southwest Airlines\uc2 ' flight attendants in December rejected a tentative agreement with the company that would have included an initial 20% pay increase followed by 3% annual raises in subsequent years.\par \par \uc2 In January, Southwest's flight attendants voted in favor of authorizing a strike for the first time, though negotiations continued. The union ratified a new four-year contract in April, which included larger pay raises. It also elected a new president who ran on a promise to repair a disconnect between union leaders and members.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Paul Kiernan, Drew FitzGerald and Alison Sider contributed to this article.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw262\pich510\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3930\pichgoal7650
