\par\par \b \uc2 Finance News: London-Listed Firms Get More Freedom on Pay\b0\par\par\uc2 By Cristina Gallardo \par \uc2 423 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 October 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B10\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 London-listed companies will be given more freedom to boost wages of their top executives, after a large investor body changed its guidance for remuneration committees.\par \par \uc2 The Investment Association\uc2 , which represents 250 large investors with stakes in businesses listed in the U.K., said Wednesday that it had simplified its principles of remuneration, following a review aimed at ensuring that the U.K. markets continue to attract high-quality companies.\par \par \uc2 The move comes after \uc2 David Schwimmer\uc2 , the chief executive of the \uc2 London Stock Exchange Group\uc2 , warned last month that London's prestige as a top financial center was at risk if executives' wages weren't increased.\par \par \uc2 Andrew Ninian\uc2 , director of stewardship, risk and task at the IA, said that the association has simplified its principles of remuneration "to demonstrate that investors want to incentivize delivery of long-term performance."\par \par \uc2 "Investors want to see companies succeed and deliver long-term returns to their shareholders, with the structure of executive pay playing an important role in driving and rewarding these results," he added.\par \par \uc2 The chief executives of the companies listed in the FTSE 100 index are being paid more than ever before, according to the High Pay Centre think tank, which estimates that they received an average 2.2% pay rise in 2023, boosting median annual pay to <pound>4.19 million, equivalent to $5.5 million.\par \par \uc2 But the gap with their peers in U.S.-listed companies has widened. Chief executives' pay at S&P 500 companies increased almost 13% in 2023 to an average of $16.3 million, according to data analyzed for the Associated Press by information services firm \uc2 Equilar\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The IA's updated principles, which are often followed by remuneration committees when setting executives' pay, now allow for companies to benchmark their wages against competitors overseas, in a bid to help London-listed businesses struggling to poach chief executives being paid higher wages elsewhere.\par \par \uc2 Remuneration committees of companies "deriving significant revenues from particular markets such as the U.S." or "competing for talent globally" would be "encouraged to set out the impact of attracting global talent on the positioning of remuneration," the updated guidance document stated.\par \par \uc2 The IA, which represents investors responsible for <pound>9.1 trillion worth of assets, also backed the use of hybrid pay schemes -- a combination of performance shares and restricted shares -- recognizing that these are more commonly offered in companies with a significant footprint in the U.S. than those listed in Britain.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020241010ekaa0000o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
