\par\par \uc2 REVIEW & OUTLOOK (Editorial)\par \b \uc2 Michigan and the EV Mandate Backlash\b0\par\par\uc2 848 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 October 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A14\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The Biden-Harris Administration keeps selling the wonders of its electric-vehicle rules and subsidies, but the politics of this supposed gift aren't turning out so well. Republicans in the swing state of Michigan are campaigning on the issue, and Democrats are suddenly on the defensive.\par \par \uc2 The Trump campaign and others are blanketing Michigan with ads targeting the Administration's EV policies. "Attention auto workers: \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  wants to end all gas powered cars," warns a new Trump campaign ad. Her "push requiring electric-only is failing big. And Michigan autoworkers are paying the price. Massive layoffs already started. You could be next."\par \par \uc2 Ms. Harris tried to counter the ads at a rally on Friday in Flint by declaring, "contrary to what my opponent is suggesting, I will never tell you what kind of car you have to drive. But here's what I will do. I will invest in communities like Flint. We will retool existing factories, hire locally and work with unions to create good paying jobs."\par \par \uc2 Translation: The government will provide reparations to auto workers whose jobs are destroyed by its climate policies.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Here's the policy background: The \uc2 Environmental Protection Agency\uc2 's new greenhouse gas emissions rules require that battery-powered and plug-in hybrid vehicles make up 32% of auto maker sales in 2027. By 2032 no more than 29% of new cars can be gas-powered. Ergo, there will be only one gas-powered model for every two electric cars on dealer lots.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Harris and Democratic Senate candidate \uc2 Elissa Slotkin\uc2  claim the Biden rule isn't an EV mandate. "No one should tell us what to buy, and no one is going to mandate anything," Ms. Slotkin says in an ad.\par \par \uc2 The reality is that there will be many fewer gas-powered cars available, and they'll cost much more owing to the government's limit on supply. As her GOP opponent, Mike Rogers, points out in a dueling ad, Ms. Slotkin voted last month to keep the Administration's mandate in place -- an accurate reference to a House resolution to overturn the \uc2 EPA\uc2  rules.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump likes to say EVs are great, if not for everyone. They're especially impractical for Americans who commute long distances and live in colder climates, which drain batteries more quickly. EVs cost about 15% to 20% more than gas-powered cars despite large sales discounts, and luxury models make up about three-quarters of EV sales.\par \par \uc2 Nonetheless, auto makers must spend tens of billions of dollars to ramp up EV production to meet government mandates. The financial pain is growing for companies as sales of gas-powered cars decline, reducing profits available to invest in the EV "transition."\par \par \uc2 -- \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  last month announced nearly 200 layoffs at its plant in Sterling Heights, Mich., citing "intense external market conditions." This followed 2,450 layoffs announced in August at its plant in Warren, Mich. CEO \uc2 Carlos Tavares\uc2  compared the EV transition to a Darwinian struggle and warned that some auto makers might not survive.\par \par \uc2 -- \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  last December cut shifts in Detroit and Toledo, Ohio, citing "the need to manage sales of the vehicles they produce to comply with California emissions regulations," which Ms. Harris has endorsed.\par \par \uc2 -- GM in December laid off 945 workers in Orion, Mich. The state had awarded the company $600 million to convert the plant to produce electric trucks, but GM has delayed its restart owing to flagging EV demand. "We are adjusting our spending plans to make sure we're capital efficient and moving in lockstep with customers," CEO \uc2 Mary Barra\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 -- \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  last year closed its Belvidere, Ill. plant and laid off 1,200 workers. In July the Administration awarded the company $335 million to retrofit the factory to make EVs. Yet \uc2 Stellantis\uc2  tapped the brakes on this plan last month amid slowing EV sales. The \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  are threatening to strike, but the union backed the Administration's EV push despite knowing it would cost jobs.\par \par \uc2 A \uc2 UAW\uc2  study in 2019 projected that EVs would kill 35,000 jobs at its plants. "The workers who are making engines and transmissions today, their jobs will be eliminated when we make a transition to electric vehicles," said \uc2 UAW\uc2  research director Jennifer Kelly.\par \par \uc2 EVs require fewer parts than internal combustion engines, and as the report explained, "EV batteries are mostly made by suppliers in other countries, with China in the lead." Michigan has lost 9,500 jobs in auto and parts manufacturing over the last five years. Most new EV investment is flowing to Sun Belt states with right-to-work laws and lower operating costs.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 All of this is creating the popular backlash that the GOP is understandably using to good political effect. The election is forcing the policy debate that Congress never had. Michigan's Senate and presidential races are tight, and if Democrats lose either one, their EV coercion will be a big reason.\par \par \uc2 (See related letter: "Letters to the Editor: Supply Chains Are Putting the Brakes on EVs" -- WSJ Oct. 15, 2024)\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020241009eka90003d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
