\par\par \uc2 PERSONAL JOURNAL\par \b \uc2 When Changing Their Jobs, Workers Neglect 401(k) Plans --- Job switchers often reset the savings rate lower on their retirement accounts without realizing it\b0\par\par\uc2 By Anne Tergesen \par \uc2 945 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 October 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A10\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Switching jobs can boost your pay and prospects. It can also drag on retirement savings.\par \par \uc2 The majority of people who change jobs wind up putting less of their pay into their 401(k)s, often without realizing it, according to new research from Vanguard Group. That is because many job switchers either forget to sign up for the 401(k) plan, or get auto-enrolled at a lower savings rate.\par \par \uc2 Over a four-decade career, that can mean as much as $300,000 less in retirement wealth for someone with average pay, \uc2 Vanguard\uc2  found.\par \par \uc2 The 401(k) was supposed to solve a key problem of pensions: Workers had to stay in a job for decades to get a decent benefit. Even though 401(k) savings remain with workers who switch jobs to seek better salaries and more fulfilling work, this research suggests people who stay put keep the most momentum in their retirement savings.\par \par \uc2 The savings rate of the median job switcher in the \uc2 Vanguard\uc2  study drops by nearly a percentage point, despite a 10% raise. Some also save less because of pay cuts or life changes.\par \par \uc2 Because the typical American changes jobs every five years or so, 401(k) savings rates can resemble a "saw-toothed pattern" of ups and downs over time rather than the steady increase financial advisers typically recommend, said \uc2 Fiona Greig\uc2 , \uc2 Vanguard\uc2 's global head of investor research and policy and co-author of the new report.\par \par \uc2 "The job switch is a time when a number of bad things can happen to retirement savings," said Greig. "It's a point of weakness in the U.S. retirement system."\par \par \uc2 Financial advisers recommend that people start saving money in 401(k) accounts early in their careers, and increase their savings rate as they get annual raises until reaching 12% to 15% of pay.\par \par \uc2 About 60% of plans that use \uc2 Vanguard\uc2 's record-keeping services automatically enroll new hires in retirement savings plans. The most popular default contribution rate is 3%. Many employers automatically increase workers' savings rates by 1 percentage point annually until reaching 10% of pay. When combined with employer matching contributions, that can get them to the recommended 12% to 15%.\par \par \uc2 But workers frequently fall back to a lower savings rate when they change employers, and can take years to bring it back up.\par \par \uc2 Carolyn Bolton, 36 years old, was saving 10% at her previous job at a nonprofit, which contributed another 15% of pay. When she switched to a more fulfilling job at another nonprofit, she took a substantial pay cut. Until she receives a raise, she plans to keep her contributions at the 2% of pay that her new employer automatically enrolled her at.\par \par \uc2 After leaving jobs, many workers also break into their old 401(k)s, often to meet emergency needs. Others roll their 401(k) money into an individual retirement account but forget to invest it.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Vanguard\uc2  research tracked 54,793 workers who moved out of one \uc2 Vanguard\uc2 -administered 401(k) plan into another between 2015 and 2022. It found:\par \par \uc2 Some 64% of job-switchers got a raise, but only 44% maintained or increased their savings rate.\par \par \uc2 More than half of auto-enrolled workers remained at the default savings rate within the first year in a new job.\par \par \uc2 In 401(k) plans that require workers to sign up on their own, nearly one-quarter of job-switchers failed to do so.\par \par \uc2 The long-term costs of slowdowns in retirement savings can be significant.\par \par \uc2 A 25-year-old earning $60,000 who is automatically enrolled at 3% of pay and raises that by 1 percentage point a year to 10% would end up with nearly $800,000 by age 65, according to \uc2 Vanguard\uc2 , which assumed a 50% employer matching contribution on the first 6% of employee contributions.\par \par \uc2 In contrast, someone who changes jobs eight times and whose savings rate falls to 3% each time would have less than $500,000, assuming the same matching contribution and 1 percentage point annual increase in savings rate.\par \par \uc2 Vanguard\uc2  is discussing with companies the benefits of automatically enrolling workers at 6% of pay, in part to reduce or eliminate savings-rate declines when changing jobs, said Kelly Hahn, head of retirement research at \uc2 Vanguard\uc2 . Another solution is for federal lawmakers or regulators to allow employers to automatically enroll new hires at the rate they had in their previous 401(k) plans.\par \par \uc2 Sarah Behr, a financial adviser in San Francisco, said about a fifth of her clients have switched jobs every couple of years. Workers who are automatically enrolled often assume, mistakenly, that the default savings rate is enough, she said.\par \par \uc2 "While changing jobs, a lot comes at you," she said. "Often, the 401(k) falls off the list."\par \par \uc2 Adam Haller, 51, said he generally kept his savings rate at around 6% over the years, despite job changes and pay raises. In 2021, the Raleigh, N.C., resident landed a new job with a big raise, and boosted his savings to roughly 10%.\par \par \uc2 After his employer filed for bankruptcy in 2022, he took a position at a mortgage lender that pays him commissions. The father of three said his income isn't yet high enough to allow him to contribute to his new employer's plan.\par \par \uc2 He figures his retirement savings balance of $420,000 would be at least $100,000 higher had he saved 10% through his career. "Changing jobs has not been as advantageous as I had hoped," he said.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020241009eka900004\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
