\par\par \b \uc2 Shipping Stocks Fall as Port Strike Ends --- Deal with U.S. dockworkers ends expectations of higher freight rates\b0\par\par\uc2 By Dean Seal \par \uc2 318 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 October 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B11\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Container-shipping stocks fell Friday after U.S. dockworkers agreed to return to work with a beefed-up contract offer, nixing for now the prospects of higher freight rates.\par \par \uc2 Shares of \uc2 ZIM Integrated Shipping Services\uc2  fell nearly 13% to $18.95, American depositary receipts of Germany's \uc2 Hapag-Lloyd\uc2  slid 15% to $77.63 and ADRs of Denmark's \uc2 A.P. Moller-Maersk\uc2  retreated 5.3% to $7.19, echoing declines in European markets. ADRs of China's \uc2 Cosco Shipping\uc2  were down 9.3% to $7.70.\par \par \uc2 All four stocks had posted sharp and steady gains over the past month as negotiations between the \uc2 International Longshoremen's Association\uc2  and port operators broke down, resulting in a strike last week that closed container ports along the East Coast and Gulf Coast.\par \par \uc2 Investors had expected the strike to increase demand for space on containerships and raise freight rates, boosting the ship operators' earnings.\par \par \uc2 The walkout ultimately lasted only three days, shorter than many observers had expected, after port employers reportedly offered a 62% increase in wages over six years, up from a prior proposal for a 50% raise.\par \par \uc2 The union and port operators said the tentative agreement would extend the prior contract, which expired at the start of last week, through Jan. 15, 2025, while the two sides negotiate on other issues, including automation on the docks.\par \par \uc2 Stifel\uc2  analysts said late Thursday that they had expected the strike to last about two weeks, given how far apart the port workers and operators were on many issues, and the White House's message that it wouldn't intervene to get the ports back open.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration privately and publicly pressed the large shipping lines and cargo terminal operators to make a new offer, according to The Wall Street Journal.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020241007eka70000e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
