\par\par \b \uc2 Hiring Blows Past Expectations --- September jobs data suggest U.S. might be headed toward soft landing\b0\par\par\uc2 By Harriet Torry \par \uc2 828 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 October 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The U.S. labor market strengthened in the weeks before Election Day, as job growth accelerated in September and the unemployment rate ticked lower.\par \par \uc2 Employers added 254,000 jobs last month, the Labor Department said Friday. That was significantly more than the 150,000 economists expected, and marked the largest monthly increase since March. The unemployment rate slipped to 4.1%.\par \par \uc2 Friday's bumper payrolls report is likely to close the door on another half-percentage-point rate cut by the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  at its next meeting in November. It should keep officials on track to lower rates by a quarter point.\par \par \uc2 The Fed is trying to engineer what is called a soft landing, in which inflation moves down without major deterioration in the labor market. Though Friday is just one data point, it suggests that the U.S. is headed in that direction.\par \par \uc2 "It puts another set of wheels under the plane in terms of assuring a soft landing," said \uc2 Gregory Daco\uc2 , chief economist at \uc2 EY\uc2 -Parthenon.\par \par \uc2 Friday's report also signaled that hiring this summer wasn't as weak as initially thought. Revised figures show employers added 72,000 more jobs in July and August combined than earlier reported.\par \par \uc2 The latest snapshot of the labor market's health comes just a month before the U.S. presidential election, where the economy and inflation are key issues for voters.\par \par \uc2 The strong jobs report could help Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . In polls, she trails former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  on the economy.\par \par \uc2 Stocks ticked higher. The Dow Jones Industrial Average rose 0.8%, or about 340 points, to 42,352.75, a record.\par \par \uc2 During much of the inflationary post-Covid-19 boom, stocks often quaked temporarily at stronger-than-expected economic data, because traders took such shocks as a sign that the Fed would tighten monetary policy more aggressively.\par \par \uc2 Investors' positive reaction Friday showed that they view good news as good news again -- even if futures contracts tied to the federal-funds rate suggest traders are now expecting a slower pace of Fed easing.\par \par \uc2 Employers added jobs at bars and restaurants and construction, as well as in sectors that are less sensitive to the economy's ups and downs like government, education and healthcare.\par \par \uc2 There were a few weak spots. Employers modestly cut head count in manufacturing, transportation and warehousing, and temporary help services.\par \par \uc2 Analysts also said that although September's jobs report was unexpectedly strong, other economic data point to a slightly less robust hiring picture. Labor Department data released earlier this week showed that the share of workers quitting their jobs each month fell to its lowest rate in more than four years in August, a sign people are more hesitant about leaving their current roles for new ones.\par \par \uc2 Wages picked up slightly last month, according to the Labor Department. Average hourly earnings rose 4% from a year earlier, the strongest increase since May. That was well above the pace of inflation, which is positive news for price-pinched consumers. The consumer-price index was up 2.5% in August from a year earlier.\par \par \uc2 "There's reason for caution, but also the resilience of the U.S. just continues to confound expectations," said \uc2 James Knightley\uc2 , chief international economist at ING. "For that to continue, it needs the Fed to continue gradually easing policy just to give the economy a little bit more breathing room to continue growing."\par \par \uc2 Inflationary pressures have eased markedly over the past two years, and the Fed's focus has shifted more to hiring than price increases. That means the jobs market will play an outsize role in Fed officials' decisions on the path of interest rates.\par \par \uc2 Conventional wisdom holds that cooling inflation comes hand-in-hand with a sharp slowdown in the labor market. However, over the past couple of years, U.S. employers have muscled through high interest rates with continued hiring, and inflation has fallen significantly. That combination, if sustained, would amount to a big win for the Fed.\par \par \uc2 "Perhaps we should reassess our analytical framework of soft landing versus hard landing and just call it what it is: a robust economic expansion," said RSM U.S. chief economist \uc2 Joe Brusuelas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Fed Chair \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  said earlier this week that officials would continue to reduce interest rates from a two-decade high to maintain solid economic growth, but that they didn't see a reason to lower rates as aggressively as they did at their most recent meeting.\par \par \uc2 The Fed's next policy meeting is Nov. 6-7. Officials will see one more employment report before then, for October. That report, which will be released Nov. 1, could be less rosy. The month has already seen a major strike by dockworkers and a continuing strike at \uc2 Boeing\uc2 . Hurricane Helene could also muddy the October jobs numbers.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Matt Grossman contributed to this article.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw553\pich384\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal8295\pichgoal5760
