\par\par \b \uc2 Pay on Docks Can Be High, but Not for All\b0\par\par\uc2 By Paul Berger \par \uc2 739 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 October 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A9\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The demand by striking U.S. dockworkers for a 77% wage increase was breathtaking in its scale even to veterans of the bare-knuckle negotiations of past port labor contracts.\par \par \uc2 Dockworkers say that behind the outsize pay demand and the ranks of high-earning union members, especially in the New York region, are thousands who earn far less loading and unloading cargo at ports in places like Virginia, Florida and Texas, where pay scales are lower and ports aren't as busy.\par \par \uc2 Tens of thousands of dockworkers from Maine to Texas went on strike Tuesday in the first coastwide walkout by the \uc2 International Longshoremen's Association\uc2  in nearly 50 years. Then late Thursday, dockworkers agreed to come back to work after port employers offered a 62% wage boost over six years, according to people familiar with the matter.\par \par \uc2 Many of the dockworkers earn six-figure salaries. Others are struggling, thanks in part to arcane labor practices.\par \par \uc2 Modern containerships are as big as skyscrapers are tall. When they pull into port, dockworkers can spend 20 hours or more unloading and loading thousands of containers filled with imports of food, clothing and electronics as well as exports of paper products and grains. When a ship leaves, it takes the work with it.\par \par \uc2 International Longshoremen's Association\uc2  President Harold Daggett says dockworkers deserve a pay hike for working through the Covid pandemic and helping the world's largest ocean shipping companies earn record profits. The carriers collectively earned more than $110 billion in 2021, according to marine consulting firm Sea-Intelligence, about triple their combined operating profit over the prior 10 years.\par \par \uc2 The port employers include some of the world's biggest shipping lines, like Denmark-based \uc2 A.P. Moller-Maersk\uc2  and Switzerland-based Mediterranean Shipping, as well as terminal operators owned by pension and infrastructure funds. They lease and run many of the private cargo-handling terminals that shuttle goods between ship and shore.\par \par \uc2 Daggett's original demand was to raise the ILA workers' base hourly rate over six years to $69 from $39.\par \par \uc2 Base pay figures can be misleading because dockworker wages vary widely depending on a worker's skill level, seniority and how busy a port is. The Port of New York and New Jersey, the busiest container port on the East Coast, handled three million more imported containers in the first seven months of the year than came into the Port of Virginia in the same period.\par \par \uc2 Because of the obscure way that East and Gulf Coast dockworkers are paid -- often via local shipping associations that represent port employers -- there is no central clearinghouse for their wages. Employer groups and the union don't divulge average wages.\par \par \uc2 Shipping industry officials say it isn't uncommon for ILA dockworkers to clear six figures. In the fiscal 2019-20 year, when ILA base pay reached $36, more than half of 3,726 dockworkers at the New York-New Jersey port earned over $150,000, according to a report by the port's regulator. Some 665 dockworkers that year earned more than $250,000.\par \par \uc2 For longshore workers on the West Coast, who work under a different union and a single pay system, the base hourly rate last year was $52.85. The \uc2 Pacific Maritime Association\uc2  says the average, full-time dockworker that year earned almost $233,000.\par \par \uc2 A 46-year-old longshoreman from Suffolk, Va., said he struggles to make $70,000 a year, even though he must make himself available seven days a week and works double-time and overtime to boost his pay packet.\par \par \uc2 "I'm relying on my wife right now and what she does to help make ends meet," he said. Some people work so much, he said, "they don't see their family just to make ends meet."\par \par \uc2 Dockworkers can boost their pay by working in bad weather or at odd hours such as evenings and weekends, as well as by putting in overtime. It isn't unusual for dockworkers to work shifts that stretch for 15- or 20-plus hours when a ship is in port.\par \par \uc2 They can boost wages by having skills as mechanics or crane operators or by managing other workers. They also earn a cut of a container royalty fee, a tonnage assessment on cargo that is charged to the ocean shipping companies.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Chao Deng contributed to this article.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw553\pich356\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal8295\pichgoal5340
