\par\par \b \uc2 Strike At Ports Threatens To Snarl U.S. Trade --- Longshoremen hit picket lines seeking big pay increase on East and Gulf coasts\b0\par\par\uc2 By Paul Berger \par \uc2 1,242 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 October 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dockworkers at dozens of U.S. ports dug in for a massive pay increase after walking off the job early Tuesday, seeking to flex their power in a strike that aims to strangle the flow of trade across much of the country.\par \par \uc2 "This is going down in history what we're doing here," Harold Daggett, the head of the \uc2 International Longshoremen's Association\uc2 , told hundreds of dockworkers on the picket lines at the East Coast's busiest port, at New York-New Jersey. "Nothing is going to move without us."\par \par \uc2 Daggett rejected an offer late Monday of a 50% wage increase over six years, a boost from an earlier proposed 40% increase in wages, along with other improvements in benefits. The ILA wants to raise the base hourly rate for its roughly 45,000 members to $69 from $39, a 77% pay increase, over six years as a condition to sit down to talks with maritime employers, according to a person familiar with the negotiations.\par \par \uc2 The White House scrambled on Tuesday to push the dockworkers and business owners back to the negotiating table, hoping a quick resolution would minimize any economic fallout that could hurt the election prospects of Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , the Democrats' presidential nominee. Both the campaigns of Harris and GOP presidential nominee \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  have been courting union workers, including in two swing states affected by the strike: Pennsylvania and Georgia.\par \par \uc2 After any wage agreement is reached, the two sides would need to bargain over thorny issues such as expanded use of automation, which the union wants to prohibit.\par \par \uc2 Dockworkers typically earn a six-figure annual salary because of work rules and overtime requirements. In the financial year that ended in 2020, more than half of 3,726 dockworkers at the Port of New York and New Jersey earned more than $150,000, according to a report by the port's regulator. About 1 in 5 dockworkers at the port earned over $250,000 that year.\par \par \uc2 Dockworkers gathered late Monday and Tuesday morning in groups several-hundred-strong, outside closed port facilities in New Jersey, blasting rock music, smoking cigars, holding placards protesting the use of automated equipment on the docks and waving U.S. flags.\par \par \uc2 "I am here to support the union and to support the middle class of America," said Matthew Dombrowski, a former Marine who now works as a crane operator. "We are fighting to go against automation. We are fighting to keep crane operators."\par \par \uc2 Daggett said his workers deserve a share in the hundreds of billions of dollars the world's largest ocean shipping companies made during the Covid-19 pandemic and more recent supply-chain disruptions caused by war in the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 "They know my number. They don't want to deal with my number. And my number is not that bad," Daggett said. The ILA represents workers on East and Gulf Coast ports.\par \par \uc2 White House officials believe the impact on consumers will be limited for now, and they have so far discounted invoking federal law to force dockworkers back to work.\par \par \uc2 The walkout shuts down some of the U.S.'s main gateways for imports of food, vehicles, heavy machinery, construction materials, chemicals, furniture, clothes and toys.\par \par \uc2 Big retailers, with their busy fall shopping season just starting to kick in, said that for now they can withstand the slowdown because they brought in products earlier than usual this year and diverted other cargoes to West Coast ports in case of a strike. But executives said a walkout lasting a week or longer would push up shipping costs and might trigger product shortages.\par \par \uc2 "Shoppers can rest assured holiday merchandise will be on shelves," said \uc2 Brian Dodge\uc2 , president of \uc2 the Retail Industry Leaders Association\uc2 , which represents stores such as \uc2 Best Buy\uc2 , \uc2 Home Depot\uc2 , Gap and \uc2 Dollar General\uc2 . "The longer this work stoppage goes on, the harder it will become to shield customers from its effects."\par \par \uc2 Susanne Waidzunas, global supply manager at \uc2 Inter IKEA Holding\uc2 , manager of \uc2 IKEA\uc2 's supply chain, said the retailer was pulling its containers that had arrived at the Port of New York and New Jersey in recent weeks as fast as possible ahead of a shutdown.\par \par \uc2 "We have learned a lot during Covid how to navigate and minimize the impact" of disruptions, Waidzunas said. "Depending on how long the strike continues, it might have an impact on a larger scale of course in the world."\par \par \uc2 A strike lasting even one week would tie up ships for much longer periods, which could exacerbate shipping delays, eat up capacity and drive up freight rates, some industry analysts warned.\par \par \uc2 About 60% of containerized trade moves through the East Coast and Gulf Coast ports where ILA dockworkers last year unloaded about $588 billion of imports, according to S&P Global Market Intelligence.\par \par \uc2 J.P. Morgan\uc2  equity analysts estimated a ports strike would cost the U.S. economy between $3.8 billion and $4.5 billion a day, some of which would be recovered once normal operations resume.\par \par \uc2 The Port of New York and New Jersey, the third-busiest container seaport in the country, handled the equivalent of more than 420,000 imported containers in July. That same month, the Port of Baltimore, the country's largest gateway for automobiles and light trucks, processed more than 34,000 new vehicles.\par \par \uc2 Descartes\uc2 , a supply chain technology provider that tracks ocean shipments, said container imports into U.S. West Coast ports expanded 22% in the first two weeks of September from the first two weeks of August. Volumes into East Coast ports declined 4% in the same period.\par \par \uc2 The potential for a strike "hasn't impacted us in terms of delivery," said Yoshika Hirata, senior director of operations at Stance, an apparel brand that makes socks and underwear and imports mostly through West Coast ports. "But what it has impacted is the cost because obviously rates are going up because of the situation."\par \par \uc2 Some wholesale importers are already raising their prices to account for the higher costs.\par \par \uc2 Tim Ryan, owner of Square 1 Farms, a Sunrise, Fla.-based importer that sells asparagus to supermarkets such as \uc2 Walmart\uc2 , \uc2 Kroger\uc2  and Wegmans, said he is having to fly in vegetables that would usually arrive by containership. He is adding about 50 cents a pound to the prices he charges stores to cover the airfreight costs.\par \par \uc2 "Either supermarkets elect to absorb that cost or they will pass it on," Ryan said.\par \par \uc2 Dockworkers on the West Coast, represented by the separate \uc2 International Longshore and Warehouse Union\uc2 , won a 32% wage increase over six years in negotiations that ended last year. The agreement also included $70 million in one-time payments, dubbed a "hero bonus" by people familiar with the contract, in a nod to work done through the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Ocean carrier earnings surged this year after Houthi rebel attacks on ships in the Red Sea forced carriers to take longer routes around the region, eating up capacity and driving up freight rates. Denmark-based shipping giant \uc2 A.P. Moller-Maersk\uc2  projected in June a $3 billion improvement in free cash flow for the year over a prior forecast.\par \par \uc2 Maersk\uc2  said the U.S. dockworkers strike will lead to delays in cargo movement, increased costs, and logistical challenges for businesses.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Liz Young contributed to this article.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw480\pich561\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal7200\pichgoal8415
