\par\par \b \uc2 A Job at \uc2 Boeing\uc2  Isn't the Ticket That It Used to Be\b0\par\par\uc2 By Sharon Terlep \par \uc2 1,267 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 October 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Three generations of Merwin family men have built their careers in \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's factories.\par \par \uc2 Tony Merwin, a 30-year \uc2 Boeing\uc2  veteran who groomed his three sons for jobs there, is considering something he once believed was unthinkable: "Right now, the way this company is, I would not steer my grandkids toward this company."\par \par \uc2 American factory jobs used to promise a middle-class life for blue-collar workers like Merwin and his sons, now in their 30s. But that is no longer true for many manufacturing workers -- even as presidential candidates \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  have campaigned on reviving such jobs.\par \par \uc2 That tension is behind the strike at \uc2 Boeing\uc2 , where the average machinist makes around $75,000 and wages haven't kept up with the cost of living.\par \par \uc2 "I've watched my kids struggle to have what they have," said Merwin, 58 years old, a parts inspector who works at \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's Auburn, Wash., plant. He will receive a pension when he retires; his youngest son won't. "The opportunity for people coming into this company is not the same as what it used to be."\par \par \uc2 Some 33,000 members of \uc2 Boeing\uc2 's largest union have been on strike since Sept. 13. Members overwhelmingly rejected a contract that would have raised pay by 25% over four years. \uc2 Boeing\uc2  sweetened its offer to a 30%, which the union said still falls short.\par \par \uc2 The union says \uc2 Boeing\uc2 , in the past decade, has raised wages by 8% -- when inflation has measured more than 46% nationally.\par \par \uc2 The Merwin family's connection to \uc2 Boeing\uc2  started with Tony's father, Ray Merwin, who in the 1960s was living in South Dakota and working at a gold mine. Ray's uncle had moved to Seattle in the early 1960s for a job at \uc2 Boeing\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Within the decade, \uc2 Boeing\uc2  would construct its sprawling Everett factory, where the company has built planes -- from the 747 to the 787 Dreamliner -- before it moved all production of the 787 to South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 When Tony was an infant, a relative called his father to join him at the factory. That move, in 1966, started nearly six decades of Merwins at \uc2 Boeing\uc2 . Tony's mother, Maria Merwin, took a job as a clerk at the jet maker.\par \par \uc2 For the young family, \uc2 Boeing\uc2  was a way out of low-income, dangerous mining work. Tony recalls growing up in a newly constructed three-bedroom rambler. As \uc2 Boeing\uc2  churned out jetliners in the suburbs of Seattle, subdivisions cropped up, populated by ranch and split-level homes.\par \par \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  moved there in 1979 and went public seven years later, birthing a wave of young tech millionaires. \uc2 Jeff Bezos\uc2  founded \uc2 Amazon.com\uc2  the following decade.\par \par \uc2 Multimillion-dollar homes filled once-open land and glittering condo towers cropped up along the lake shore. By 2020, Seattle and the surrounding area had among the nation's priciest homes and least-affordable communities.\par \par \uc2 The median worker at \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  in 2023 made nearly $200,000. The average wage at an \uc2 Amazon\uc2  warehouse is $22 an hour -- no college degree required -- compared with about $21 on average to start on the line at \uc2 Boeing\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 At 23, Tony was hired as a wing line mechanic on a military plane. He jumped between jobs at \uc2 Boeing\uc2 . The work provided a middle-class life, where bonuses and overtime paychecks went toward vacations, band trips and college tuition for his daughter.\par \par \uc2 "Everybody was able to pay their bills so, when a bonus came, it was, 'I'm going to take a vacation, or I'm going to build a back deck,'" he said. "Now everybody is depending on a bonus as a stopgap for what they can't afford."\par \par \uc2 By 1999, married with four adolescents, Tony paid $155,000 for a three-bedroom on a large corner lot. His children remember him working often but also having a lot.\par \par \uc2 "We were able to survive without any problems," said his second son Andy Merwin, who has worked at \uc2 Boeing\uc2  for 13 years. "I remember asking my dad how he did that."\par \par \uc2 Andy, 38, initially was an electrician and later moved into a planning job, represented by a different union. He later got an engineering degree. A father of four, Andy makes $105,000 a year including overtime and lives about 30 miles away. To pay their mortgage, the family also relies on income from his wife, who teaches piano.\par \par \uc2 The young family is considering moving to Utah where the cost of living is lower and Andy said he thinks he could find another aerospace job.\par \par \uc2 Tony got divorced in 2014 and moved into an apartment with his brother. He recalls the sticker shock at the $1,300 monthly rent. Tony remarried and, a few years later, the couple moved into a two-bedroom apartment. In 2019 -- their rent closing in on $1,800 -- the couple decided to buy their own place. His wife is disabled and can't work, he said.\par \par \uc2 They bought a single-wide mobile home on a spacious tract, roughly 45 minutes from work. It cost $235,000. Tony, who made $120,510 this past year, including overtime and a bonus, said they could have swung something slightly higher-end on less land, but opted for the mobile home so they could keep a trailer and two classic Mustangs, for which storage had become too costly.\par \par \uc2 Almost as shocking to Tony as home prices was the $22 turkey leg at the state fair. He remembers paying $11 last time the couple went in 2018.\par \par \uc2 "This sounds like first-world problems," he said. "But this was never an issue before. I used to be able to take care of a family of six in a really nice house. The company is not that ticket anymore."\par \par \uc2 For Tony's sons, \uc2 Boeing\uc2  remains a path to homeownership and stability. But it comes with increasing trade offs. His youngest son, Patrick Merwin, 36, joined the Air Force at his father's urging, as had his older brothers. Unlike them, Patrick said, he hoped to avoid \uc2 Boeing\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 After his six-year military stint, newly married, Patrick took a job at an aerospace maintenance operation in North Carolina. He worked there a few years until getting laid off. His family counseled him to return home -- and join \uc2 Boeing\uc2 . Patrick went to work for a contractor and eventually got hired by the jet maker at its 737 factory in Renton, Wash. At $20 an hour, \uc2 Boeing\uc2  paid $9 an hour less than his contractor gig, but he could work up the pay scale.\par \par \uc2 Patrick said he saved money by living with his sister and working close to 500 overtime hours his first year. Now, six years after his hire, he makes $46.71 an hour, the top rate for an aircraft test technician. He pays a mortgage on a three-bedroom home, now worth more than $500,000, about an hour south of Renton.\par \par \uc2 He still works overtime, including most weekends, but he switched to a lower-paying shift so he can spend more time with his children and drive them to and from school.\par \par \uc2 "We are able to stay afloat," Patrick said. "But not necessarily to thrive."\par \par \uc2 Tony, Patrick and Tony's oldest son Josh Merwin, who also works at \uc2 Boeing\uc2 , recently stood together on a picket line. Tony reminisced about joining a picket line as a teenager in 1977 with his father.\par \par \uc2 "This company has zero future," Tony said, "if it cannot go back to being able to attract the best and brightest."\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw380\pich323\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal5700\pichgoal4845
