\par\par \b \uc2 Dockworkers Strike North and South\b0\par\par\uc2 By Paul Berger \par \uc2 658 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 October 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Dockworkers walked off their jobs at dozens of ports from Maine to Texas at midnight, launching a strike that threatens to rattle the American economy five weeks ahead of the presidential election.\par \par \uc2 Members of the \uc2 International Longshoremen's Association\uc2 , which represents 45,000 dockworkers at East Coast and Gulf Coast ports, began picketing early Tuesday at cargo terminals that handle more than half of American import and export volumes as the contract with port employers expired.\par \par \uc2 Port employers, pressed by Biden administration officials to resolve the impasse, raised their offer on wages to a 50% increase over six years, from an earlier 40% increase, along with other improvements in benefits in the 24 hours before the strike deadline.\par \par \uc2 The ILA is seeking a 77% wage increase over six years as a condition to sit down to talks with maritime employers, according to a person familiar with the negotiations.\par \par \uc2 The walkout shuts down some of the country's main gateways for imports of food, vehicles, heavy machinery, construction materials, chemicals, furniture, clothes and toys.\par \par \uc2 The longshore workers walked off their jobs after the union refused to meet with the group representing employers unless they first agreed to workers' wage demands.\par \par \uc2 The White House is intervening as business groups and Republican lawmakers have stepped up calls for the administration to invoke federal law to keep the ports open.\par \par \uc2 Big retailers, with their busy fall shopping season just starting to kick in, say that for now they can withstand the slowdown because they brought in products earlier than usual this year and diverted other cargoes to West Coast ports in case of a strike. But executives say a walkout lasting a week or longer would push up shipping costs and might trigger product shortages.\par \par \uc2 "Shoppers can rest assured holiday merchandise will be on shelves," said \uc2 Brian Dodge\uc2 , president of \uc2 the Retail Industry Leaders Association\uc2 , which represents stores such as \uc2 Best Buy\uc2 , \uc2 Home Depot\uc2 , Gap and \uc2 Dollar General\uc2 . "The longer this work stoppage goes on, the harder it will become to shield customers from its effects."\par \par \uc2 Susanne Waidzunas, global supply manager at \uc2 Inter IKEA Holding\uc2 , the company that manages \uc2 IKEA\uc2 's supply chain, said the retailer was pulling its containers that had arrived at the Port of New York and New Jersey in recent weeks as fast as possible ahead of the shutdown.\par \par \uc2 "We have learned a lot during Covid how to navigate and minimize the impact" of disruptions, Waidzunas said. "Depending on how long the strike continues, it might have an impact on a larger scale of course in the world."\par \par \uc2 A strike lasting even one week would tie up ships for much longer periods, which could exacerbate shipping delays, eat up capacity and drive up freight rates, some industry analysts warn.\par \par \uc2 The growing competition for space on ships bound for the West Coast ports was already driving up freight rates in the period leading up to the walkout.\par \par \uc2 The potential for a strike "hasn't impacted us in terms of delivery," said Yoshika Hirata, senior director of operations at Stance, an apparel brand that makes socks and underwear and imports mostly through West Coast ports. "But what it has impacted is the cost because obviously rates are going up because of the situation."\par \par \uc2 "We actually built our calendar with quite a bit of buffer to account for this," she said.\par \par \uc2 Some wholesale importers are already raising their prices to account for the higher costs.\par \par \uc2 Tim Ryan, owner of Square 1 Farms, a Sunrise, Fla.-based importer that sells asparagus to supermarkets such as \uc2 Walmart\uc2 , \uc2 Kroger\uc2  and Wegmans, said he is having to fly in vegetables that would usually arrive by containership. He is adding about 50 cents a pound to the prices he charges stores to cover the higher airfreight costs.\par \par \uc2 "Either supermarkets elect to absorb that cost or they will pass it on," Ryan said.\par \par \uc2 ---\par \par \uc2 Liz Young contributed to this article.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020241001eka10003t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
