\par\par \b \uc2 EXCHANGE --- Science of Success: Need a CEO? Hire the Intern. Seriously -- Just Do It. --- Sometimes a company like \uc2 Nike\uc2  needs a leader who understands it deeply\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ben Cohen \par \uc2 1,233 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 September 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par B2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 After the call that would transform his life, \uc2 Elliott Hill\uc2  hung up the pay phone, stuffed everything he owned in his car and drove from Ohio to Tennessee so he could start his dream job at \uc2 Nike\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But when he walked into the company's Midwest regional sales office for his first day of work, there was just one problem. Hill's new boss told him that it wasn't exactly a job. It was actually going to be a six-month internship.\par \par \uc2 "An internship?" he thought.\par \par \uc2 It was an inauspicious start, but Hill lasted longer than six months at \uc2 Nike\uc2 . In fact, he would spend his entire career at the same company. He started in 1988 and got promoted every few years for the next few decades. By the time he retired in 2020, he was president of \uc2 Nike\uc2 's consumer division.\par \par \uc2 But he was recently lured back for one last job -- and this time, it wasn't an internship.\par \par \uc2 He was just hired as the next CEO.\par \par \uc2 When \uc2 John Donahoe\uc2  abruptly resigned as \uc2 Nike\uc2 's chief executive last week, his ouster marked the end of a rough stretch in which the company lost its edge -- and billions of dollars in market value. Before he was named CEO, he'd never worked at \uc2 Nike\uc2 . He's being replaced by someone who's basically his exact opposite.\par \par \uc2 Hill, a 60-year-old company lifer who calls \uc2 Nike\uc2  "a core part of who I am," is the latest example of a curious business archetype: the Intern CEO.\par \par \uc2 Doug McMillon\uc2  started at \uc2 Walmart\uc2  as an hourly associate unloading trailers. \uc2 Mary Barra\uc2  worked for \uc2 General Motors\uc2  as a student before she took a full-time job on the assembly line inspecting fenders and hood panels. \uc2 Christian Klein\uc2  hauled monitors from the basement of \uc2 SAP\uc2 's headquarters to the engineers and developers upstairs. "And not flat screens," he told me. "The heavy ones."\par \par \uc2 All three have been with their companies ever since. All three are now leading those companies.\par \par \uc2 When \uc2 Ursula Burns\uc2  was a summer intern at \uc2 Xerox\uc2  in 1980, it was completely unimaginable to her that she would one day be CEO.\par \par \uc2 "I didn't know that we had a CEO," she told me.\par \par \uc2 But after getting her master's degree in mechanical engineering, Burns started moving up the \uc2 Xerox\uc2  corporate ladder. She worked in the research lab, business planning, the C-suite, global manufacturing and internal operations. Then she ran the whole company from 2009 to 2016.\par \par \uc2 Starting at the bottom of a company and climbing to the very top has always been an improbable career path. But these days, it feels closer to impossible.\par \par \uc2 The median job tenure of U.S. workers has dropped below four years, the lowest number in decades, according to newly published federal data, as job-hopping has become increasingly common for talented young employees. Mobility is hot. Loyalty is not. Which means Intern CEOs might be a dying breed.\par \par \uc2 They may not have firsthand knowledge of how other companies function, but they do have institutional knowledge of their own. They remember which ideas worked and why. They also remember every cockamamie strategy suggested by people who knew precisely nothing about the company but pretended to know it all. What they lack in perspective, they make up for with experience. Where outsiders see problems, interns see promise. And they have the credibility to sell their vision for change when it's necessary.\par \par \uc2 "If you're going through a transition in the company," Burns said, "having someone who understands the heart and soul of the place is valuable."\par \par \uc2 Nike\uc2  is currently going through one of those transitions.\par \par \uc2 As my colleague Inti Pacheco has explained, the company made a series of costly strategic missteps in recent years, like sprinting away from retail stores and toward e-commerce. Meanwhile, increased competition and stalled innovation resulted in meager sales, sunken morale and something of an identity crisis for the sneaker giant.\par \par \uc2 This company founded by runners even missed out on America's latest running boom. The only thing more blasphemous for \uc2 Nike\uc2  would be losing Michael Jordan to Hoka.\par \par \uc2 Nike\uc2  fell behind under Donahoe, whose experience at the company before he took over was limited to a board seat. He spent the formative part of his career at Bain, where he started as a lowly associate consultant and became CEO. He left for the top jobs at \uc2 eBay\uc2  and \uc2 ServiceNow\uc2 . Nearly five years ago, he traded software for shoes and found himself running \uc2 Nike\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In other words, he was the epitome of a Consultant CEO. He's being replaced by the quintessential Intern CEO.\par \par \uc2 Hill's first job out of college was an entry-level position on the training staff of the \uc2 Dallas Cowboys\uc2 , where he worked for a year before pursuing his master's degree in sports administration. While he was at \uc2 Ohio University\uc2  for graduate school, he took a class on sports marketing and wrote a paper about one of his favorite brands: \uc2 Nike\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So when a \uc2 Nike\uc2  executive named Tim Joyce visited campus, Hill arranged for a meeting, put on his best suit and begged for a job. Then he kept pestering Joyce for months. As graduation approached, Hill promised to never call him again if there was no way he would get hired. Otherwise, he was going to keep calling until Joyce said no. He finally said yes on a Friday. Hill started on Monday.\par \par \uc2 "I never told my mom it was an internship," he said on the Fortitude FW Podcast last year. "I told her I got hired."\par \par \uc2 Once he managed to get his foot in the door, he never let it close on his Nikes. As an intern, he packed boxes in the warehouse and picked up phones whenever the office needed help with customer service. "I pretty much did everything they asked me to do -- and then some," he says.\par \par \uc2 At the time, he was still paying off his student loans. But with that attitude, he turned his internship into a proper job as an apparel sales representative. Over the next two years, he put 120,000 miles on his Chrysler minivan.\par \par \uc2 There was nothing even remotely glamorous about this work: The man was going to mom-and-pop shops in Texas and Oklahoma peddling Lycra.\par \par \uc2 Hill was so far down the org chart and so far away from corporate headquarters that it took a decade for \uc2 Nike\uc2  co-founder \uc2 Phil Knight\uc2  to have any clue who he was.\par \par \uc2 Even today, when the subject turns to the company's chairman emeritus and largest individual shareholder, Hill sounds like an intern ready to fetch coffee. In reverential tones, he calls Knight the most inspirational person he's ever met -- and "the person I tried the hardest to make proud." And he did: Knight himself led the push bring Hill back.\par \par \uc2 Now the company is in the hands of somebody who cares about \uc2 Nike\uc2  and its culture so deeply that he cries when he talks about how much the brand means to him -- somebody willing to do everything he's asked and then some.\par \par \uc2 It worked for Hill as an intern. It might just work for him as the Intern CEO.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020240928ek9s0000x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
