\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Harris Champions Government Policy to Support U.S. Manufacturing\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jeanne Whalen, Justin Lahart and Richard Rubin \par \uc2 740 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 September 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  pledged to use the might of the government to support domestic manufacturing and help the U.S. beat China in the industries of the future, embracing a policy that puts federal intervention at the heart of her economic plan.\par \par \uc2 Harris on Wednesday said that if elected president, she would support a new tax credit that will invest tens of billions of dollars in domestic manufacturing. The credit would aim to create jobs in cutting-edge fields such as biotechnology and aerospace production, and to strengthen traditional industries such as iron and steel, Harris said in a policy document.\par \par \uc2 President Biden has long championed such policies, dedicating hundreds of billions of dollars to supporting the country's manufacturing of computer chips, electric vehicles, batteries and other high-tech goods.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has also placed American manufacturing at the core of his economic campaign, though the former president aims to use import tariffs and a targeted corporate tax cut to support domestic producers. Harris's campaign has criticized Trump's proposals, saying the costs of the new tariffs would likely get passed along to American consumers, leading to higher prices.\par \par \uc2 Harris is aiming to pay for the $100 billion in tax credits over a decade by raising taxes on U.S. companies' foreign earnings. The plan calls for Congress to implement the global corporate minimum tax agreement, campaign advisers said.\par \par \uc2 Harris struck a moderate and pragmatic tone in a speech in Pittsburgh on Wednesday, referring to herself as a capitalist who believes in a market economy. Trump has worked to paint her as a left-wing ideologue, often referring to her as "Comrade Kamala."\par \par \uc2 "One of the recurring themes of American history is that when we make an intentional effort to invest in our industrial strength, it leads to extraordinary prosperity and security -- not only for years but for generations," Harris said, before ticking off President \uc2 Abraham Lincoln\uc2 's investments in the transcontinental railroad, President \uc2 Dwight Eisenhower\uc2 's interstate highway construction and President \uc2 John F. Kennedy\uc2 's support for the space race.\par \par \uc2 Her plans are largely an extension of Biden's yearslong effort to use government tools and finances to boost key sectors of the economy. The approach, known in economic parlance as "industrial policy," is also increasingly supported by some Republicans, who have relaxed their free-market convictions to help the U.S. compete with China, which uses state-backed loans, equity and government subsidies to support its manufacturers.\par \par \uc2 Robert Barbera, an economist at \uc2 Johns Hopkins University\uc2 , sees some justification in adopting the kind of industrial policy Harris is leaning toward -- some manufacturing capacity is necessary for economic security. "You don't want to be in a position where you can't get the chips you need to put in your cars and computers because China gave you the middle finger," he said.\par \par \uc2 But Barbera also thinks that the emphasis both Harris and Trump have put on manufacturing jobs merely taps into sentimentality for bygone days. "There is a nostalgic sense about their importance because manufacturing jobs were the higher paying jobs and they were highly unionized and they were trendsetters," he said.\par \par \uc2 Factory employment has cut an erratic path in recent years. It grew in the first two years of Trump's administration -- in January 2019, there were a seasonally adjusted 462,000 more manufacturing jobs than there had been when he took office in January 2017. But they plateaued over the course of the next year, and then fell sharply when the pandemic hit. In January 2021 there were 178,000 fewer manufacturing jobs than there had been four years earlier. As of August, the U.S. has added 739,000 manufacturing jobs during the Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 Even so, manufacturing now accounts for just 8.1% of U.S. jobs, a slightly lower share than at the end of former President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 's time in office. In 1950, that share averaged 31%.\par \par \uc2 Michael Hicks\uc2 , an economics professor at \uc2 Ball State University\uc2  in Indiana, said the costs to taxpayers of industrial policy often outweigh the benefits. Job creation from such programs, he said, could be relatively low because high-tech factories tend to be highly automated.\par \par \uc2 Harris was speaking at the Economic Club of Pittsburgh, to an audience likely receptive to the idea of restoring American manufacturing.\par \par {\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw553\pich310\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal8295\pichgoal4650
