\par\par \b \uc2 U.S. News: Plant Closure Hits Hard in Rural Virginia --- Laid-off Boar's Head workers need jobs as manufacturing in region disappears\b0\par\par\uc2 By Chao Deng \par \uc2 985 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 September 2024\par \uc2 The Wall Street Journal\par \uc2 J\par A3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2024 Dow Jones & Company, Inc. All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 GREENSVILLE, Va. -- A line of about a dozen laid-off workers of Boar's Head formed outside the redbrick assembly hall before doors opened at 9 a.m. It was the second day of one-on-one severance discussions with the company, and for many newly unemployed, the stress of finding a new job was taking over.\par \par \uc2 Lamont Ferguson arrived, ducking out of rain showers, manila envelope in hand. As he returned to his mother's car, the father of six took a moment to ponder where they would go next. He held an advertisement for a hiring event by \uc2 Reser's Fine Foods\uc2  that wasn't until later in the month. "We'll go file for unemployment," he said, resignation in his voice.\par \par \uc2 For decades, the Boar's Head deli meat plant has been one of few economic pillars holding up this rural, downbeat part of southern Virginia. This plant was shut down after it was linked to a deadly listeria outbreak. The company has begun recalling seven million pounds of sausages, ham, frankfurters and bacon made in the plant, where inspectors found multiple health violations, from mildew and insects to black mold on walls and equipment, according to \uc2 U.S. Agriculture Department\uc2  records.\par \par \uc2 The sudden loss of the county's biggest employer has shocked the community and put 500 people, mostly Black workers, out of jobs. Many are scrambling to figure out how to pay their bills and whether they can even stay in a region battered by waves of layoffs and closed businesses.\par \par \uc2 Many former workers say they will now have to commute long distances in an area where manufacturing has long been hollowed out, starting in the 1990s when the elimination of trade barriers invited global competition. Textiles and furniture-making went by the wayside, joining tobacco production as industries that have largely faded from the area.\par \par \uc2 "The job opportunities there are not good," said Fletcher Mangum, founder of an economic consulting firm in Richmond and an adviser to the Virginia governor's office. Mangum placed much of the cause on the \uc2 North American Free Trade Agreement\uc2 , a 1992 agreement that lifted trade tariffs on most goods produced by Canada, Mexico and the U.S. "Manufacturing has had ups and downs but it never quite recovered from the losses of Nafta."\par \par \uc2 Business accounting, computer technology and other professional services are thriving in northern Virginia, with its proximity to Washington, D.C.\par \par \uc2 The state as a whole has struggled in recent years. As the host of the \uc2 Defense Department\uc2  headquarters and home to several naval bases, it was disproportionately hit by federal funding cuts in 2013. Since the financial crisis of 2008, Virginia has largely lagged behind the nation in employment growth.\par \par \uc2 The more-rural southern and western parts of the state have over the past decade suffered the most. Year-over-year employment in Greensville County, where the Boar's Head plant is located, has mostly underperformed that of the state since 2017, according to the Labor Department's Bureau of Labor Statistics. Some forestry and logging survived, although wages in those industries are lower paying and jobs are sometimes limited to family-owned businesses.\par \par \uc2 The local union said the severance packages were "well above and beyond what is required under the law" and that it was coordinating with the state to provide support for the former workers. Greensville County has encouraged people to attend job fairs and told them how to file for unemployment. About 200 of the 500 workers lived in Emporia and its surroundings; some were also based in Jarratt, the nearest town to the meat-processing facility.\par \par \uc2 "Everybody is a little bit in shock," said county economic development director Natalie Slate.\par \par \uc2 Manufacturing makes up about 30% of the economy in Greensville, compared with about 7% for the state, according to federal data. The plant accounted for about a fifth of its private-sector jobs; other top employers are a prison and the public school system.\par \par \uc2 The economic challenges in Greensville have followed resident Marvin Tiller most of his career. Before getting laid off from Boar's Head, the 51-year-old worked for two companies that shut operations in the area more than a decade back -- he was a crane operator for a steel company called Emporia Foundry, and a delivery man for Fujifilm Pharmaceuticals.\par \par \uc2 "The economy is really tough right now with the cost of living going up," said Tiller. He had worked at Boar's Head for 10 years and was now thinking about getting out of factory work altogether to try to join \uc2 Amazon\uc2  or a firm that, to him, felt more stable.\par \par \uc2 At CornerStone Crossroads, the closest convenience store to the factory, the cooks and cashier had come to know and befriend Boar's Head workers who ordered burgers, fries and bacon biscuits regularly. These days the lunchtime crowds have thinned out considerably.\par \par \uc2 At the company event Tuesday, workers huddled to console each other. One man in a Boar's Head sweatshirt who had been with the company for 20 years teared up.\par \par \uc2 Brian Newell, 46, said his girlfriend lost her job at Boar's Head after working there 10 years. He now feels pressure to cover financial expenses for the two of them. He works full time as a production supervisor at a nearby metal chassis factory, while also putting in a few hours a week at \uc2 Dollar General\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Another employee, who declined to give her name, emerged from the building after learning, to her surprise, that she would get to keep the job she had for nearly three decades, albeit at another Boar's Head facility in Petersburg, about 30 miles north.\par \par \uc2 The majority of workers, along with their family members, weren't so lucky.\par \par \uc2 Document J000000020240925ek9p0003d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw300\pich40\picscaley125\picscalex125\picwgoal3600\pichgoal480
